\par\par \b \uc2 Chinese Exporters Helping Pressure Dollar-Yuan -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,255 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2018\par 02:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 2010 ET - As chatter about the \uc2 PBoC\uc2  possibly stepping in to cool the yuan swirls, ING greater \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist \uc2 Iris Pang\uc2  doesn't see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  happening. Dollar-yuan has been hit in part by Chinese exporters selling greenbacks ahead of Chinese New Year, she notes. But last month's 3% gain is still cause for pause, especially those who need to exchange dollars for yuan. Even if folks think the yuan may rise further, Pang isn't convinced it's enough to force the \uc2 PBoC\uc2 's hand as that may run counter to the optics of liberalizing the exchange market. She thinks \uc2 PBoC\uc2  may limit "hot money" coming into China, primarily in its bond markets, if the dollar hits CNY6.2 this week. It's around CNY6.29. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2003 ET - Australian building approvals dropped 20% in December from November, with apartments plunging close to 40%. But that after growth through November was 13%, so the sequential slide probably isn't as alarming as it appears. In trend terms, approvals were down just 1.7%, so that should restore some sanity to the data set. And Australia is still building a lot of apartments while large projects can come in or go out of the data. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1954 ET - Speculators potential putting "significant" pressure on dollar-yuan could prompt the \uc2 PBoC\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Mark Williams, chief Asia economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . The yuan's jumped 0.7% onshore yesterday versus the dollar to CNY6.2920. The pair was around CNY6.20 for months before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's August 2015 devaluation of the yuan. But Williams also notes the yuan's gains being driven by the dollar's weakness, not big, broad gains versus a host of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He says the \uc2 PBoC\uc2  could relax controls on outward capital flows or directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1910 ET - At a CFR event in New York, Canadian Foreign Minister \uc2 Chrystia Freeland\uc2  was pressed by audience members on what is the country's "red-line" Nafta issues. She mentioned 2, the first being the US demand on autos to have more regional content, including a minimum level for the US. Canada offered an informal proposal that broadens the definition of what counts as car content, but US Trade Representative \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2  dismissed the idea this week, warning it could lead to job losses on the continent. Freeland also cited Nafta's Chapter 19 provision, which lets expert, binational panels settle disputes on tariffs between Nafta parties. "It was probably from the Canadian perspective the single biggest thing the original" created, she said, adding Canada could be open to modifying or improving the process. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 1902 ET - Though the pace is still expected to slow, strength in housing credit in Australia continues to surprise. Annualized system-wide credit growth of about 4.5% in the last quarter was again underpinned by growth in housing loans, which showed 5.5% growth in the latest period, Macquarie says. That suggests regulatory restrictions have yet to really bite the banks, the investment bank says. One reason for the current strength may be that customers are rolling over existing interest-only debt with their existing bank, Macquarie suggests. For now, it says trends are supportive for retail banking and sector valuations look attractive. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 1858 ET - Canada Foreign Minister \uc2 Chrystia Freeland\uc2  tells a New York audience that the biggest challenge in Nafta talks is to mesh 2 competing visions. She contends Canada wants to update the pact and expand the country's trading relationship with the US while the Trump administration wants to "shrink" trade ties in some areas. She described talks, which completed the 6th round in Montreal this week, as taking place on 2 tracks. Things are "going well" on so-called bread-and-butter trade issues while "there is more distance" on 5 of the most-contentious US demands for a new Nafta, such as on autos. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 1845 ET - Japanese stocks are likely to rally thanks to the yen's decline overnight, following 6 straight losing sessions. The USD/JPY is at 109.25, up from 108.69 as of Wednesday's Tokyo stock market close. Still, any modest gain in the Nikkei might not be big enough to put the index above its 25-day moving average of 23509. Domestic corporate earnings are eyed for any further gains. Nikkei ended down 0.8% Wednesday at 23098.29. Nikkei futures open up 190 points at 23295 on SGX.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1830 ET [Dow Jones ] -- Aussie house prices were 0.3% weaker in January from December, with the largest market of Sydney leading the way with a 0.9% retreat, says \uc2 Corelogic\uc2 . While the size of the fall isn't great, the breadth of it is. The Sydney fall isn't inconsiderable. It is enough to puncture consumer confidence a little. Australians look to property for wealth creation, and get jittery when the market turns south. With interest rates at record lows, there's not much hope the \uc2 RBA\uc2  will step in to revive the market. james.glynn@wsj.com ; @JamesGlynnWSJ\par \par \uc2 1640 ET - The FOMC said that it expected inflation to continue to move higher through this year. It also pointed out that inflation expectations were moving higher. This statement is likely to make bond traders feel justified in their shorts, says ANZ. A major question for markets now is whether the rise in yields can finally stall the decline in the USD, it adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1522 ET - January figures from Realestate.com show the New Zealand housing market remains softer than a year ago and, as a result, price growth is likely to remain muted in the near term, \uc2 ASB\uc2  says. With the introduction of numerous investor measures around the corner such as the removal of negative gearing, investor demand is likely to continue to wane over 2018, they say. "Overall, we see a much softer outlook for the housing market over 2018 relative to recent years, both in terms of activity and prices." Still, they think price growth will remain marginally positive as a result of the ongoing undersupply of houses compared to population growth. (ben.collins@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1510 ET - Price-action is still suggestive of a market that is short the Kiwi, \uc2 Australia and New Zealand Banking Group\uc2  says, though there was some respite once 74 cents was breached as markets jostle ahead of the FOMC. "Up around these levels we still believe things look stretched, but it [NZD/USD] remains at the whims of global forces," the bank says. The cross was at 0.736 early in Asia on Thursday. (ben.collins@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1429 ET - With the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  signaling it will remain on its current course of gradual rate increases, the market is increasingly pricing in the next rate hike for March. Shortly after the Fed's policy announcement, federal-funds futures, used by traders to place bets on the course of interest rates, showed an 86% chance of an interest-rate increase in March, up from 74% one week ago and 52% at the end of December. (akane.otani@wsj.com; @akaneotani)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 31, 2018 20:11 ET (01:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180201ee2100079\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
