\par\par \b \uc2 Asian companies count losses, hatch ways to cope with weak dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2018\par 09:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Jan 24 (Reuters) - A relentless decline in the dollar is putting pressure on earnings of Asian exporters, pushing some to consider moves such as renegotiating sales contracts, but it has also created a buffer against rising raw material and foreign debt costs.\par \par \uc2 Having endured the dollar's 10 percent decline against major world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2017, exporting firms, such as Japanese and Thai automakers, South Korean electronics and technology firms and Chinese chip makers, are readying for further gains in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 South Korea's won is already at a 3-year high against the dollar, China's yuan at its highest in two years and the Japanese yen has scaled 110-per-dollar.\par \par \uc2 At Japan's \uc2 Nissan Motor Corporation\uc2 , every one point move in the yen-dollar exchange rate hits operating profit by 17 billion yen ($154.8 million). The company has assumed an average dollar/yen rate of 108 for the 2017 fiscal year, which ends this March.\par \par \uc2 "Our overall expectation for the 2017 fiscal year is that \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  will negatively impact our operating profit by 60 billion yen, across all \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not just dollar," said Nick Maxfield of \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's communications department.\par \par \uc2 South Korea\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2 , the world\u8217\'20\'19s second-biggest memory chip maker, said a 10 percent drop in the dollar versus the Korean won could cause the firm to take a hit of about 300 billion won ($280.6 million) through its dollar-denominated foreign assets.\par \par \uc2 An official from the silicon industry branch of the \uc2 China Nonferrous Metals Industry Association\uc2 , which is responsible for over 200 silicon firms, said long-term overseas orders that Chinese traders had signed last year had become less lucrative because of the weakening dollar and due to rising costs from tighter domestic environmental protection policies.\par \par \uc2 "This has put pressure on traders and is causing profit margins to gradually shrink," he said, noting China accounts for more than 70 percent of global silicon volumes.\par \par \uc2 "People won't be making money...and so, to survive, they may take the risk of evading taxes."\par \par \uc2 In Thailand, the regional production and export hub for the world's top carmakers, a strong baht has added to the burden from a drop in Middle Eastern vehicle demand.\par \par \uc2 "The strong baht impacts our profits so we have had to undertake cost management measures, such as negotiating with buyers," said Chatchai Taveesakulvadchara, executive vice president at Toyota Motor Thailand, which commands a third of the domestic market.\par \par \uc2 GLASS HALF FULL\par \par \uc2 But a weaker dollar environment is not all bad news with local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength due in large part to robust demand for Asian-made goods and a broad-based global economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Strong domestic consumption also remains a significant help for manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , for example, expects its Thai sales to rise nearly 25 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 Under such conditions, policymakers remain sanguine about firmer Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 South Korea and Thailand have both said they will leave \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to market forces, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to contain volatility.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tweaked its exchange rate settings to allow the yuan to align more closely with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its trading partners. The yuan rose about 6.5 percent against the dollar in 2017.\par \par \uc2 A weaker dollar also supports local purchasing power, a boon for both consumers and manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 For some businesses, the strength in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps offset higher costs of imported raw materials and their foreign debt payments.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nippon Paper, for instance, said the value of its raw material imports exceeds that of its exports so a weaker U.S. dollar would normally be positive for the company.\par \par \uc2 A one point rise in the yen against the dollar, therefore, would roughly equate to a 200 million yen earnings boost over a six-month period, a Nippon Paper spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 Both \uc2 LG Display\uc2 , an Apple supplier, and Korea's \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  say a weak dollar affects exports but also reduces the costs of servicing foreign debt.\par \par \uc2 "The strengthening won has a negative aspect on overseas sales but at the same time an effect in reducing the won-converted amount of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt," a Hyundai Motor spokeswoman said in an email reply to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Brenda Goh and SHANGHAI NEWSROOM, Ritsuko Ando in TOKYO, Joyce Lee and Ju-Min Park in SEOUL, Chayut Setboonsarng in BANGKOK; Writing by Vidya Ranganathan; Editing by Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-1-24T09:41:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180124ee1o00bdr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
