\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan firms as China's congress begins, market ignores weaker fixing\b0\par\par\uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2017\par 06:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Winni Zhou and David Stanway\par     SHANGHAI, Oct 18 (Reuters) - China's yuan firmed against the\par U.S. dollar on Wednesday, with markets awaiting policy\par initiatives from the key Communist Party congress and shrugging\par off a weaker official midpoint fixing.\par     The once-every-five-years congress began with little\par reaction to early comments from Chinese President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xi Jinping\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par that China will deepen market-oriented reform of its exchange\par rate as well as its financial system.\par     Xi is expected to lay out new policy initiatives and further\par consolidate his power for his second five-year term.\par     Market participants said they expect a certain degree of\par stability in the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the period when Beijing\par holds the high-profile meeting, which will close on Oct. 24.\par     The offshore yuan also edged up after the United States on\par Tuesday refrained from naming China a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator. \par     Prior to market opening, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set its\par midpoint for trading at 6.5991 per dollar, 108 pips or 0.16\par percent weaker than the previous fix of 6.5883.\par     Traders said Wednesday's official guidance rate largely\par matched market forecasts, following a rise in the dollar\par overnight in overseas markets.\par     But the official fixing did not drag spot prices lower. The\par onshore spot market opened at 6.6160 per dollar and\par was changing hands at 6.6108 at midday, 142 pips firmer than the\par previous late session close but 0.18 percent weaker than the\par midpoint. \par     Market participants said traders were building up long\par positions in the yuan, while some companies continued with their\par seasonal dollar buying. Such corporate demand for the greenback\par capped gains in the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     A dealer at a Chinese bank in Shanghai said traders\par increased buying interest in the yuan on the first day of the\par 19th Party Congress. The yuan has strengthened more than 5\par percent against the dollar so far this year.\par     Pan Gongsheng, head of the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par regulator said on Wednesday that he expects the yuan exchange\par rate to have a more stable foundation after the Party Congress,\par adding the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has "basically exited" from its regular\par yuan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market.\par     The United States refrained from naming \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par manipulator although it remained critical of the Chinese\par government's economic policies ahead of a planned visit to\par Beijing by President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     The semi-annual \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  report said no\par countries deserved the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator label, but it kept\par China on a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "monitoring list" despite a fall in China's\par global current account surplus since 2016.\par     The Thomson Reuters/HKEX Global CNH index, which\par tracks the offshore yuan against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a\par daily basis, stood at 95.77, firmer than the previous day's\par 95.72. \par     The global dollar index fell to 93.476 from the\par previous close of 93.486. \par     Offshore yuan was trading 0.09 percent firmer than the\par onshore spot at 6.605 per dollar. \par     Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards contracts\par (NDFs), considered the best available proxy for\par forward-looking market expectations of the yuan's value, traded\par at 6.7595, 2.37 percent weaker than the midpoint.\par     One-year NDFs are settled against the midpoint, not the spot\par rate.\par     \par     The yuan market at 0353 GMT: \par     \par     ONSHORE SPOT:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint      6.5991   6.5883    -0.16%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.6108   6.625     0.21%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    0.18%              \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change YTD                       5.08%\par  Spot change since 2005                25.20%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par     Key indexes:\par      \par  Item            Current     Previous  Change\par                                        \par  Thomson         95.77       95.72     0.1\par  Reuters/HKEX                          \par  CNH index                             \par  Dollar index    93.476      93.486    0.0\par  \par     \par     \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.605     0.09%\p