\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-Beijing prepares to stand yuan policy on head\b0\par\par\uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2017\par 08:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.)\par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Sept 11 (Reuters Breakingviews) - China is considering whether to stand its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy on its head. After years spent crushing yuan short positions, officials worry they've overheated the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But capping this rally will be tricky, diplomatically and economically. Better, perhaps, to let the bulls run a while longer.\par \par \uc2 As the yuan fell against the dollar starting in 2014, the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved to stop capital flight, using a mixture of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and administrative edicts. It was largely successful. The yuan is up 7.5 percent this year, reserves are stable, and private investment is back.\par \par \uc2 A bit too successful, perhaps. With a strong economy and a weak dollar, yuan bulls suddenly look scarier than the bears. Last Friday the yuan touched 6.43 per dollar \u8211\'20\'13 its strongest rate since 2015 - after gaining over 2.3 percent in just two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Graphic: China has historically had to worry more about speculative inflows than outflows as the yuan has appreciated: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://reut.rs/2eXmHqk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://reut.rs/2eXmHqk}}}\par \par \uc2 Apparently in reaction, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased restrictions on offshore forwards \u8211\'20\'13 making it easier to short yuan \u8211\'20\'13 and signalled discomfort with the pace through conservative settings to its guidance rate. Sources told Reuters some officials worry the strong yuan is hurting China's export sector, a big employer, after August exports disappointed.\par \par \uc2 So-called "hot money" inflows have historically been a bigger headache than capital flight, scrambling trade data and helping inflate asset bubbles. Today, thanks to recent programmes opening China's stock and bond markets to foreign investors, it's never been easier for quick cash to flow in.\par \par \uc2 But overreaction is always a risk. Suppressing the exchange rate to support exports wouldn't play well in Washington, given friction over imbalanced trade. It would also waste an opportunity to resume reform. The tough campaign against outflows, which entailed sneaky attacks against shorters in overseas markets and suppression of outbound M&A, applied a lot of tarnish to Beijing's reform credentials. Putting that process in reverse would not inspire; better to lower more barriers to outflows.\par \par \uc2 Nor is hot money so threatening. Chinese exporters are practised at hedging \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. The last three years proved the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has gotten much better at managing liquidity in different exchange rate environments. It could be even be helpful: the government could sterilise inflows via the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, then reinvest them in "Belt and Road" infrastructure overseas.\par \par \uc2 Either way, one day the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will hike, and the dollar will rise again. Sometimes it's best to do nothing.\par \par \uc2 On \uc2 Twitter{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/petesweeneypro"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/petesweeneypro}}}\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS\par \par \uc2 - China's yuan has strengthened 7.5 percent against the dollar this year, more than erasing the 6.5 percent loss posted in 2016. Last week it passed a key 6.5 per dollar level for the first time since May 2016.\par \par \uc2 - Chinese policymakers are beginning to worry about the rallying yuan as exporters come under strain, Reuters reported on Sept. 8, citing policy insiders.\par \par \uc2 - The country's exports rose by 5.5 percent from a year earlier in August, below market expectations.\par \par \uc2 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to scrap reserve requirements for financial institutions settling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward yuan positions with effect from Sept. 11, four sources with direct knowledge of the matter told Reuters. These requirements were previously put into place to discourage traders from taking short positions against the yuan.\par \par \uc2 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also begun to set the daily guidance rate in a weaker position than indicated by other market movements, indicating it may be trying to dampen further strengthening.\par \par \uc2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can click on\par \par \uc2 - SIGN UP FOR BREAKINGVIEWS EMAIL ALERTS: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bit.ly/BVsubscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://bit.ly/BVsubscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Quentin Webb and Katrina Hamlin)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-9-14T06:22:39.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170911ed9b002mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
