\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China c.bank relaxes yuan hedging rules as  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  strengthens, capital outflows ease\b0\par\par\uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2017\par 11:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * China scraps required reserves for settling forward yuan positions\par \par \uc2 * Also stopped requiring foreign banks to put aside reserves for offshore yuan deposits in China\par \par \uc2 * Analysts say \uc2 PBOC\uc2  wary of further appreciation (Adds context, more analysts' comment)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Sept 11 (Reuters) - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday scrapped two measures that were put in place to support the yuan when it was under significant selling pressure, suggesting Beijing is anxious to quash one way bets on the yuan as outflows ease and exporters face strain.\par \par \uc2 The move comes as the yuan bounced sharply this year to hit a near two-year peak on the dollar last week, giving authorities the confidence to relax their grip on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had stumbled badly in 2016 and raised risks to economic stability.\par \par \uc2 With China's economy humming along at a solid pace and putting to bed concerns of a sharper slowdown, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to pursue a more neutral yuan rate with the potential for increased two-way volatility, analysts said in the wake of the rules changes.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to convey the idea that China conducts a dynamic policy making process," Commerzbank analysts Hao Zhou and Lutz Karpowitz wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 "Against this backdrop, the capital control policies could be eased somewhat if there is no undesired market turbulence following the removal of the reserve requirements."\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  has scrapped reserve requirements for financial institutions settling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward yuan positions, it said in comments emailed to Reuters on Monday morning. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has also stopped requiring foreign banks to put aside reserves for offshore yuan deposits in China. Both changes are effective immediately.\par \par \uc2 Reuters, citing sources with direct knowledge of the matter, had reported these changes earlier.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's statement said the changes took into consideration current market conditions, adding that the yuan strength against the dollar this year reflected an improving Chinese economy.\par \par \uc2 LOWER APPETITE FOR APPRECIATION\par \par \uc2 Markets were quick to react, with the yuan losing ground despite the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  setting a higher midpoint of 6.4997 per dollar, strengthening it beyond the key psychological 6.5 level for the first time since May 2016.\par \par \uc2 The offshore and onshore yuan were both trading weaker than the 6.5 level on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Still, around 6.52 to the dollar the onshore yuan was not too far off a 21-month high at 6.4478 set on Friday, underpinned by capital curbs, broad dollar weakness and the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's tighter control of the midpoint.\par \par \uc2 At the official local close on Monday, the onshore spot yuan was up around 6.5 percent so far this year, which is about the same losses it suffered in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent policy signals "seem to point to reduced comfort with the ongoing pace of appreciation".\par \par \uc2 They highlighted a shift in the "counter-cyclical" factor in yuan fixings that had seen it turn more reactive to market appreciation pressures, potentially pointing to a reduced propensity to accommodate much more strength in the yuan.\par \par \uc2 The analysts were referring to the mysterious "counter-cyclical" factor that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  added to their calculations of the yuan's daily reference point to curb price swings in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sources have told Reuters that Chinese policymakers are beginning to worry about the strength of the yuan as exporters come under strain, risking a hit to the economy ahead of an important Communist Party gathering in the autumn.\par \par \uc2 All the same, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap it could expose \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to accusations of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation by U.S President Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 "One interpretation is that the pace of CNY appreciation has taken the PBoC, as well as the market by surprise," said Iris Pang, economist at ING, in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 "We believe that CNY appreciation will continue, but at a slower pace from now on." (Reporting Elias Glenn and Winni Zhou; Editing by Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-9-11T10:36:47.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170911ed9b009fi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
