\par\par \b \uc2 Foreign Ownership of Malaysia's Debt Falls Again -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,413 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2017\par 07:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0504 GMT - Foreign ownership of Malaysian government-debt securities fell for a 3rd month in August, those the month's MYR700 million ($166.9 million) of net outflows was much less than July's MYR2.3 billion, according to the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Maturity-driven outflow asides, Maybank says foreign buying in the domestic bond market has improved since mid-August. While some outflows are still possible in coming months, Maybank doesn't expect large maturity-driven outflows as some selling was "brought forward" during the selloff seen from Nomvember-March. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT - Though the PBoC has set the midpoint of daily trading higher for 11-straight sessions, the longest run since the yuan's hard peg to the dollar was dropped in 2005, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't want steep appreciation and is liable to step in if the dollar falls to CNY6.45, says Eddie Cheung, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  in Hong Kong. The yuan notched its biggest gain versus the dollar in 12 years last week, edging below CNY6.5 and hitting near-2-year highs. In that environment, local firms are liable to want to convert dollars earned overseas into yuan, perhaps driving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher still. The greenback is up 0.4% versus the yuan onshore 0.2% in offshore trading as the PBoC has made some move to rein in the recent rally. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0428 GMT - The head of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  says she expects South Korea's economy to expand 3% this year, faster than the 2.7% growth projected in April. IMF Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  says the country's economy will probably grow 3% also next year. The \uc2 IMF\uc2  chief, who met with Korean leaders and economic officials, says she is optimistic about the country's economy that is resilient to external shocks. The Seoul government in July also upgraded its growth estimate to 3% for this year, from its earlier 2.6% growth forecast. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com; @kwanwoo)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT - Rising urbanization will drive large-scale infrastructure development in those parts of India the next decade, notes BMI Research. Currently, non-urban infrastructure projects account for nearly 85% of activity by value. More money going toward city-focused projects would follow a pattern seen in other emerging markets, and initiatives like the Smart Cities Mission and AMRUT are early signs of the shift, BMI adds. It adds most infrastructure activity will continue to be heavily concentrated in areas of large and dense populations in states such as Maharashtra, Gujarat and Andhra Pradesh. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT - After rising nearly 6% versus the Korean won in a near-uninterrupted run higher the past 2 months, RBC says now's the time to short the offshore yuan against the won. That as moves the past week suggest \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reining in yuan appreciation by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market, setting the dollar-yuan fix higher than moves in major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  imply and accumulating value-adjusted \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the investment bank adds. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT - China has now fixed the yuan stronger against the dollar for 11 straight sessions -- the longest such streak since authorities ended the yuan's hard peg against the dollar in 2005. Monday's reference rate for the dollar against the yuan was 6.4997, which set the yuan weaker than where it ended against the dollar in onshore trading Friday. In recent action, the yuan fell 0.6% against the greenback; one dollar bought 6.4981 yuan, compared with CNY6.4617 at the close of trading Friday. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 0220 GMT - China's move to remove the reserve requirement for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards will save trading costs and make the yuan more flexible and market-driven, says CICC. But it also says that the move is unlikely to reverse market sentiment for the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose rate will be determined by domestic economy. CICC says Beijing could consider phasing out its capital control measures in response to the recent surge of the yuan. It may start with loosening control over residents' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases, CICC says. (grace.zhu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT - The first rung on the Australian property ladder is a big step, and fewer people have made the transition from renter to buyer since the global financial crisis, a paper released by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  says. But it also notes those who do get on the ladder are more financially stable than past cohorts. So while "generation rent" is an important trend for the country, the paper says the additional money needed for a deposit is also a sign of financial discipline that's associated with fewer subsequent difficulties. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT - EUR/GBP Intraday: Watch 0.9090. The pair is clearly in a down trend, and remains under pressure below its nearest resistance at 0.9145. Both the 20-period and 50-period moving averages are heading downward, and should call for further downsides. In addition, the relative strength index broke down a rising trend line, and is now below its neutrality area at 50. Therefore, as long as the resistance at 0.9145 is not surpassed, the risk of the break below 0.9090 remains high. Alternative scenario, above 0.9145, look for further upside with 0.9170 & 0.9200 as targets. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized 0investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT - EUR/JPY Intraday: Turning up. The pair bounced off its key support at 129.45, which should limit any downside room. The relative strength index is turning up, and broke above its neutrality area at 50. Besides, the 20-period moving average is now below the prices, and plays a support role. To sum up, as long as 129.75 holds on the downside, look for a new bounce to 130.40 & 130.65 in extension. Alternatively, below 129.75, expect a new pullback to 129.45 & 129.10 as targets. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized 0investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT - USD/CAD Intraday: Upside prevails. The pair remains bullish above 1.2115, and is likely to post a new rebound. The 20-period moving average has turned up, and has also crossed above the 50-period one (a bullish signal). In addition, the relative strength index is heading upward, calling for a new rise. In which case, as long as 1.2115 is not broken, likely advance to 1.2175 & 1.2205 in extension. Alternatively, below 1.2115, expect a return to 1.2060 & 1.2000 as targets. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized 0investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT - Japan's machinery orders for July indicate corporate capital spending remains broadly sluggish despite bright spots in areas like autos and transportation. Machinery orders excluding the ship and power sectors rose 8% from June after falling each month in 2Q. The Y853 billion ($7.9 billion) in July was the 4th largest of the past year.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 11, 2017 01:04 ET (05:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170911ed9b0005u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
