\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China May  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves rise more than expected on weaker dollar, capital controls\b0\par\par\uc2 834 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2017\par 12:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments by c.bank adviser)               \par \par \uc2 * Reserves reach a 7-month high of $3.054 trillion               \par \par \uc2 * China 'very successful' in containing outflows - analyst               \par \par \uc2 * C.bank adviser says \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  system should be reformed               \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, June 7 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose in May for a fourth consecutive month and by more than markets had expected, as stringent capital control measures and a weakening in the dollar helped staunch outflows.               \par \par \uc2 Reserves rose $24 billion in May to a seven-month high of $3.054 trillion, compared with an increase of $21 billion in April, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Wednesday.               \par \par \uc2 It was the first time since June 2014 that reserves climbed for four months in a row, and the biggest gain since reserves moved back above the closely watched $3 trillion level in February.               \par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters had expected reserves to rise $10 billion.               \par \par \uc2 A weaker U.S. dollar boosted the value of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and assets held by China in May, the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) said in a statement explaining the rise.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  supply and demand was basically balanced in the month, it added.               \par \par \uc2 In May, the dollar recorded its largest monthly percentage loss against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since January.               \par \par \uc2 Tighter restrictions on taking funds out of the country imposed in recent months continue to keep a lid on capital outflows, said Zhou Hao, a Singapore-based analyst at Commerzbank.               \par \par \uc2 "From their perspective, they have done a very successful job."               \par \par \uc2 Apart from valuation effects, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 ' Julian Evans-Pritchard said \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also have been purchasing small quantities of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in May after having sold \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the sagging yuan for 18 straight months.               \par \par \uc2 "This a major shift in policy that has been achieved thanks to an easing of capital outflows," he said.               \par \par \uc2 "Given our forecast for China\u8217\'20\'19s May trade balance (due out on Thursday), today\u8217\'20\'19s figures still point to capital outflows of around $10 billion last month. But this is down from $30 billion in April and $65 billion at the end of last year."                              \par \par \uc2 TURNING THE TIDE?               \par \par \uc2 In April, net \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales by the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 fell to the lowest in nearly two years as capital outflows eased in the face of strict regulatory curbs and a pause in the dollar's rally.               \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said last month that China's current cross-border capital flows are stabilizing, but debt risks and signs of slowing economic growth have forced policymakers to keep a tight leash on outflows.               \par \par \uc2 China burned through nearly $320 billion of reserves last year but the yuan still fell about 6.5 percent against the dollar, its biggest annual drop since 1994.               \par \par \uc2 After steadying early this year, the yuan suddenly bolted to near seven-month highs in recent weeks after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  changed the way it calculates its official daily reference point to quash expectations of further depreciation and outflows as the U.S. raises interest rates.               \par \par \uc2 The uncharacteristically rapid appreciation engineered by Chinese authorities were also seen as a show of strength and a warning to speculators after the country's credit rating was downgraded by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  on May 24.               \par \par \uc2 The moves have prompted some investment banks to scale back views of yuan weakness this year, with some now saying it could end 2017 relatively unchanged.               \par \par \uc2 In further evidence that Beijing remains concerned about a resurgence of outflows, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator announced last Friday that Chinese banks would be required to report daily on bank card holders' overseas transactions exceeding 1,000 yuan ($147).               \par \par \uc2 To be sure, veteran China watchers have cautioned that Chinese companies and individuals would find ways around the capital controls eventually.               \par \par \uc2 Indeed, French investment bank \uc2 Natixis\uc2  said in a report that its capital flow tracker for China showed outflows for the second quarter will rise somewhat, reversing the trend in the first quarter.               \par \par \uc2 But while moves to stabilise the yuan may have reassured China's financial markets, they have come at a price.               \par \par \uc2 Persistent depreciation fears along with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tighter capital controls and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have dealt a setback to Beijing's ambitions to make the yuan a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  advisor said China should urgently reform its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate system or risk impeding the economy and plans for the yuan to play a larger role on the global stage.               \par \par \uc2 Huang Yiping, a member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy committee, told a forum in Beijing that an inflexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate would limit the opening of the economy and yuan internationalisation.               \par \par \uc2 The yuan has slipped to the seventh-most actively traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for global trade finance, transaction service provider SWIFT said.               \par \par \uc2 Some Western firms have also been concerned about their ability to remit profits and dividends from China.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-6-7T11:57:27.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170607ed6700dil\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
