\par\par \b \uc2 Trump Treasury Department Declines to Label Any Country a  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Manipulator\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Talley \par \uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2017\par 23:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  sharply criticized China's exchange-rate policy on Friday, though it stopped short of labeling the Asian trade giant a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 "China has a long track record of engaging in persistent, large-scale, one-way \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the Treasury Department said in its semiannual report on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies of major U.S. trade partners. Although Beijing has allowed the yuan to slowly appreciate in recent years and actively fought depreciation recently, its past \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "imposed significant and long-lasting hardship on American workers and companies."\par \par \uc2 "Treasury will be scrutinizing China's trade and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices very closely, especially in light of the extremely sizable bilateral trade surplus that China has with the United States," it added.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  reversed course on a promise that he had made both on the campaign trail and from the White House: that he would designate China as violating international exchange-rate rules, pushing down the yuan to gain a competitive advantage.\par \par \uc2 The report has traditionally been used as a diplomatic tool to prod other countries whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies were deemed a threat to U.S. industries. The latest report's censure of China and other countries -- including South Korea and Germany -- could be used in the future as a pretext for new tariffs.\par \par \uc2 "Treasury is committed to aggressively and vigilantly monitoring and combatting unfair \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices," the report said.\par \par \uc2 Preserving a two-decade precedent, no country was named a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 With Friday's about-face was an acknowledgment by Mr. Trump and his team that Beijing has been propping the yuan up over the last two years, instead of pushing it down as the president had alleged.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has sought to prevent a rapid [yuan] depreciation that would have negative consequences for the United States, China, and the global economy," the Treasury Department said in its report.\par \par \uc2 Building debt problems and a slowing economy has put downward pressure on the yuan, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to burn through $1 trillion, or a quarter of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, few Western economists disagree Chinese authorities used an undervalued exchange rate to help fuel its rise to the No. 2 economy in the world. A cheaper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes products less expensive to produce and more attractive to buyers overseas. That was an essential factor in making China the world's biggest manufacturing base, but cost the U.S. and other countries millions of jobs.\par \par \uc2 China wasn't alone in being targeted in the Treasury Department's latest report.\par \par \uc2 Repeating past criticisms under the Obama administration, the Treasury department also kept China, Japan, South Korea, Taiwan, Germany, and Switzerland on a special "monitoring list" that flags trade partners with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other economic policies deemed to be a risk to the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ian Talley at ian.talley@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 14-04-17 2145GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020170414ed4e0005o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
