\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves stay above $3 trillion after small March rise\b0\par\par\uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2017\par 12:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Reserves at end-March reach $3.009 trillion\par \par \uc2 * March increase was nearly $4 bln, less than forecast\par \par \uc2 * Pause in dollar rally, capital controls seen trimming outflows\par \par \uc2 * Data comes out as Xi and Trump meet in Florida (Adds comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator, analyst)\par \par \uc2 By Cheng Fang and Sue-Lin Wong\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, April 7 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stayed above $3 trillion in March, during which a pause in the dollar's rally aided Beijing's efforts to contain capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 Reserves rose $3.96 billion during March - less than the $5 billion predicted in a Reuters poll - to total $3.009 trillion, compared with $3.005 trillion at the end of February.\par \par \uc2 In January, reserves slipped below $3 trillion for the first time in nearly six years, but in February they moved back above that line with their first rise in eight months.\par \par \uc2 The March increase marks the first time reserves had increased two consecutive months since April 2016.\par \par \uc2 Julian Evans-Pritchard of \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  said the way reserves were broadly stable in March "suggests that the recent easing of capital outflows has allowed the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step back from \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 "The obvious follow-up question is whether this halt to official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a temporary phenomenon or a more permanent shift," he wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 "Our hunch is that it will be temporary," asserting that the key factor in curtailing outflows was the pause in dollar appreciation rather than tighter capital controls."\par \par \uc2 LEADERS TALK IN FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator, in a statement on its website on Friday, said pressure on capital outflows eased somewhat in the first quarter.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are expected to further stabilise, the regulator said, adding that the monthly change in reserves' total is also stabilising.\par \par \uc2 The March reserves data came out as Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  is meeting with U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Florida, and one week before a \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report - which could label trading partners as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators - is due.\par \par \uc2 Xi urged cooperation with the United States on trade and investment.\par \par \uc2 China's State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE), the regulator, said last week that pressure from capital outflows eased somewhat in 2016 and there will be greater flexibility in the yuan's exchange rate in 2017.\par \par \uc2 The regulator also said that authorities will take measures to attract capital inflows this year.\par \par \uc2 TIGHTER FLOW SCRUTINY\par \par \uc2 In February, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold the smallest amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in nine months, supporting the government's assertions that capital outflows were easing amid tighter scrutiny of cross-border flows.\par \par \uc2 China has tightened rules on moving capital outside the country in recent months as it seeks to support the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stem a slide in reserves.\par \par \uc2 It burned through nearly $320 billion of reserves last year but the yuan still fell about 6.5 percent against the dollar, its biggest annual drop since 1994.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the yuan has been steady against the dollar. However, the meeting between U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  is expected to have an impact on yuan's value in the medium term.\par \par \uc2 Gold reserves value fell to $73.74 billion at the end of March, from $74.376 billion at end-February, data published on the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  website also showed.\par \par \uc2 Recent remarks by senior government officials seem to indicate that China is not firm in keeping its reserves above the $3 trillion mark, but prefers a gradual drop.\par \par \uc2 China does not have a "bottom line" for either the yuan exchange rate against the dollar or \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, a senior \uc2 PBOC\uc2  official told Reuters in March.\par \par \uc2 Trump had said during the U.S. election campaign that he would declare China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator on his first day in office.\par \par \uc2 But now \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy experts say that the Trump administration looks unlikely to formally declare China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator shortly after the U.S. leader meets Xi.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Cheng Fang and Sue-Lin Wong; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-4-7T11:24:39.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170407ed4700ca6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
