\par\par \b \uc2 China's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Takes a Twist Ahead of Trump-Xi Meeting\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saumya Vaishampayan \par \uc2 1,071 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2017\par 03:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 As China's leader prepares to head to the U.S. this week, there's a new twist in the persistent argument that China is keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially low against the dollar. The yuan has recently been rising.\par \par \uc2 The gains have been small -- the yuan is up 1% against the dollar so far this year -- but began shortly before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was inaugurated as president. The rise could complicate a central criticism that Mr. Trump has leveled against China: that it is manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  downward at the expense of the U.S. to help bolster exports and its economy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump and Chinese leader \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  are due to meet Thursday.\par \par \uc2 China for years had been criticized by other countries for its heavy-handed efforts to keep the yuan undervalued, even while it made moves to open its markets to free trading. Then came a period of appreciation which led to the government to suddenly devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August 2015, causing a global market selloff.\par \par \uc2 Since Mr. Trump took office, Chinese authorities have relied on capital controls and a broadly weakening dollar to keep the yuan in a narrow range against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 China achieved the recent calm with relatively mild nudges. In late 2015 and early 2016, the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling its dollar reserves to support the yuan. This year the authorities resorted to a combination of measures, including heightened capital controls and increases of domestic interest rates, analysts and investors say.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's decline against major trading partners other than the U.S. suggests an effort to maintain global trade advantages while reducing political friction with the U.S., China's second-largest partner in goods trading behind the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has fallen more than 2% against a basket of China's major trading partners, which include the U.S., the \uc2 EU\uc2  and numerous Asian countries including Hong Kong, Japan and South Korea, according to data published by the \uc2 EU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Letting the yuan strengthen against the dollar while weakening against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps China "achieve the objective of export competitiveness and reflation in the economy while at the same time avoiding that negative spillover effect," said Roland Mieth, emerging-markets portfolio manager for Pacific Investment Management Co. in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices have long been a source of tension between the two biggest economies.\par \par \uc2 Throughout his campaign for the presidency, Mr. Trump and his surrogates repeatedly accused China of manipulating the yuan in a way that boosted the U.S. trade deficit and destroyed U.S. jobs. Global investors feared new U.S tariffs.\par \par \uc2 But the administration's tone on trade has been more conciliatory in recent weeks. Last month, U.S. officials signaled they would accept only modest changes to the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2 , which Mr. Trump previously blasted as "the worst trade deal" ever.\par \par \uc2 Accordingly, financial markets have been buoyed by wagers that Mr. Trump won't follow through on tough talk that only a few months ago sparked fears of a global trade war.\par \par \uc2 "The relative stability or even mild strength against the dollar certainly provides for a less contentious backdrop for the Trump-Xi meeting coming up later this week," said Eswar Prasad, a Cornell University professor and a former head of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's China division.\par \par \uc2 In mid-April, the Treasury Department is set to release its semiannual report on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies of the U.S.'s major trading partners, and China is on its monitoring list, which tracks countries that run large trade surpluses with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Many economists and investors say that China doesn't meet the Treasury's criteria to be named a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator because its current-account surplus has shrunk and it has been burning through its reserves to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than letting it fall. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was undervalued until 2013, some analysts say, but its recent economic and trade weakness has led the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a more overvalued position.\par \par \uc2 An index of the yuan against a broad group of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  published by a branch of \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at its lowest since the basket was introduced more than a year ago, as China pledged to decouple the yuan from the dollar and let it move on market forces.\par \par \uc2 The White House is exploring a new tactic to discourage China from undervaluing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost exports by designating the practice of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation as an unfair subsidy.\par \par \uc2 By keeping the yuan from falling further against the dollar, the Chinese want Mr. Trump to "know that they're willing to play ball on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and trade issues so that the bilateral economic relationship can be maintained," Mr. Prasad said.\par \par \uc2 China's exchange rate is a major concern in the U.S., while issues such as excess capacity in the global economy are "multilateral issues" that matter more to other countries, former Treasury secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2  said last week at a panel in New York held by the \uc2 National Committee on U.S.-China Relations\uc2 , a nonprofit organization.\par \par \uc2 Among its major trading partners, China runs a trade deficit with Japan, South Korea and Taiwan, suggesting that China imports more goods from these economies than what they buy from China. China runs a surplus against the \uc2 EU\uc2 , Vietnam and Singapore, but to a lesser extent than its surplus with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 For much of 2017, the yuan's value against the dollar has been higher in offshore markets, where it trades more freely, than in domestic markets, suggesting that broader market expectations of yuan depreciation have receded for now.\par \par \uc2 "It's politically induced stability," said Claire Dissaux, head of global economics and strategy at \uc2 Millennium Global Investments Ltd\uc2 ., a London-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investment firm.\par \par \uc2 However, over the long run, economists say the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still under pressure to weaken against the dollar, as China's current-account surplus continues to narrow along with its slowing economy. Chinese households and companies are still eager to find ways to swap their yuan-denominated savings into overseas assets.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dissaux said: "Capital controls are never sustainable."\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 05-0