\par\par \b \uc2 Eclectica Asset Management Profits From \uc2 China  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2 's Battle for Yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gregor Stuart Hunter \par \uc2 945 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2017\par 08:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG--As \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  battled speculators betting against the yuan last year, one hedge fund profited from the tension.\par \par \uc2 London-based Eclectica Asset Management made money from trades that paid off whenever there was a rise in the cost of borrowing yuan in Hong Kong, the main market where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is traded outside of mainland China, according to co-founder Hugh Hendry.\par \par \uc2 Other hedge funds have taken aggressive positions betting against the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past two years, including Kyle Bass' Hayman Capital, which set up a fund last year to bet on a collapse in the yuan.\par \par \uc2 But while the yuan dropped 6.2% last year against the dollar, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  slowed its fall, largely by selling down its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in yuan markets outside of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ward off speculators aiming to profit from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide.\par \par \uc2 Those regular \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped to squeeze offshore borrowing rates for the Chinese yuan sharply higher, making it expensive for traders to wager against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But instead of betting that the yuan would keep falling, Eclectica made trades that benefited from these surges in interest rates, constructing its bet by buying and selling derivative contracts known as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards.\par \par \uc2 "We've benefited from not sharing the most dire apocalyptic scenario that most of the other managers have," Mr. Hendry said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 The Eclectica hedge fund, launched in 2002, is best known for making bets on global macroeconomic events--Mr. Hendry's most high-profile success to date was a more-than 30% return in 2008, during the depths of the global financial crisis. Other bets in its portfolio currently include ones that position the fund for an economic recovery in Japan, and the breakup of the eurozone.\par \par \uc2 The yuan is unusual among global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in that it has two exchange rates against the dollar. Within China, the yuan-dollar rate is fixed each morning and allowed to trade within a 2% band on either side of the fix. That market is still largely closed to foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 Since 2010, China has also allowed the yuan to trade more freely in offshore markets. Speculators trading in Hong Kong who expect the yuan to fall are able to 'short' the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --by borrowing yuan short-term and then selling it, hoping to buy it back when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value has dropped.\par \par \uc2 Eclectica's yuan strategy, though, was based on its view that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to realign the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's two values if they deviated too far out of line with each other.\par \par \uc2 On several occasions last year, the cost of borrowing yuan overnight in offshore markets rocketed upward, due to a sudden lack of available funds. Market participants attributed these episodes to the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  buying up yuan, using the Hong Kong branches of state-owned Chinese banks as its proxy.\par \par \uc2 By driving up interbank yuan borrowing rates, known as Hibor, the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made it less attractive for speculators to bet against the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Eclectica meanwhile set up trades to take advantage of the Hibor's volatility when set against much more stable U.S. dollar interest rates, using \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards.\par \par \uc2 For example, the fund might purchase a contract to buy yuan in a month's time and sell U.S. dollars. Simultaneously, it would purchase another contract of equal value to buy dollars and sell yuan in a year's time. At times when Hibor surged upward, it would drive up the gap between the value of the two contracts, earning Eclectica a profit.\par \par \uc2 The effect of the trade is that the fund avoids taking a view on the dollar-yuan exchange rate, but is instead betting on yuan borrowing rates--creating a trade similar to an interest rate swap.\par \par \uc2 Eclectica's trade proved particularly profitable in December, when overnight yuan interest rates in Hong Kong regularly reached double-digit levels--an unusually high cost of borrowing in most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "We're more of a view that China can muddle on and muddle through but it does require these \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Mr. Hendry said.\par \par \uc2 Eclectica's flagship hedge fund generated about 60% of its income during December from its yuan market bet, according to Mr. Hendry. That helped the fund overcome losses from earlier in the year on its stock trading in Europe and Japan and bond trading in Latin America. Eclectica Asset Management manages around $200 million in total.\par \par \uc2 The CF Eclectica Absolute Macro Fund, a version of the hedge fund which is open to retail investors and mirrors its strategy, returned 4.9% during December, according to a fund fact sheet. The fund, which has $35.5 million in assets, closed the full year down 0.5% after returning 4.4% in 2015.\par \par \uc2 However, it has proven to be consistently hard to make money from Eclectica's trade as it relies heavily on volatility in yuan interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Chinese yuan borrowing rates have collapsed this year, after the overnight rate hit a high of 61.333% in early January. The equivalent rate was 1.76283% on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "If we didn't take that trade off in January and February we'd have lost more money than we'd made in December," Mr. Hendry said. "We're looking for opportunities to put it back on again."\par \par \uc2 Anjani Trivedi\par \par \uc2 contributed to this article\par \par \uc2 Write to Gregor Stuart Hunter at gregor.hunter@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 10-03-17 0724GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020170310ed3a0005a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
