\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-China Jan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves fall below $3 trillion for first time in nearly 6 years\b0\par\par\uc2 1,039 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2017\par 13:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * China \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fall more than expected, breach key level\par \par \uc2 * Defense of weakening yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  burning through reserves\par \par \uc2 * Tighter controls slowing but not stopping illegal outflows\par \par \uc2 * Markets unsure how much longer China can both support yuan and expend reserves\par \par \uc2 * Risk of devaluation looms even as frictions with U.S. grow (Adds quote from Citi on the trade-off China's policymakers are facing)\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Feb 7 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves unexpectedly fell below the closely watched $3 trillion level in January for the first time in nearly six years, though tighter regulatory controls appeared to making some progress in slowing capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 China has taken a raft of steps in recent months to make it harder to move money out of the country and to reassert a grip on its faltering \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even as U.S. President Donald Trump steps up accusations that Beijing is keeping the yuan too cheap.\par \par \uc2 Reserves fell $12.3 billion in January to $2.998 trillion, more than the $10.5 billion that economists polled by Reuters had expected.\par \par \uc2 While the $3 trillion mark is not seen as a firm "line in the sand" for Beijing, concerns are swirling over the speed at which the country is depleting its ammunition, sowing doubts over how much longer authorities can afford to defend both the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its reserves.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts fear a heavy and sustained drain on reserves could prompt Beijing to devalue the yuan as it did in 2015, which could throw global financial markets into turmoil and stoke political tensions with the new U.S. administration.\par \par \uc2 While Beijing quickly downplayed the fall below the $3 trillion level, the breach could bolster China's argument that it not deliberately devaluing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ahead of the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 's semi-annual report in April on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, the January decline was much smaller than the $41 billion reported in December, and was the smallest in seven months, indicating China's renewed crackdown on outflows appears to be working, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 Economists expect more forceful policing of existing regulatory controls after the latest slide, though China's financial system is notoriously porous, with speculators quickly able to find new channels to get funds out of the country.\par \par \uc2 "With \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves below $3 trillion, we can expect capital controls as well as tightening yuan liquidity to continue, as the authorities try to avoid a further drawdown," said Chester Liaw, an economist at Forecast Pte Ltd in Singapore, referring the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise hike in short-term interest rates on Friday.\par \par \uc2 While the world's second-largest economy still has the largest stash of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by far, it has burned through over half a trillion dollars since August 2015, when it stunned global investors by devaluing the yuan.\par \par \uc2 The yuan fell 6.6 percent against a surging dollar in 2016, its biggest annual drop since 1994.\par \par \uc2 The crackdown is threatening to squeeze legitimate business outflows from China as well, with some European companies reporting recently that dividend payments have been put on hold and Chinese firms having a tougher time winning approval for overseas acquisitions.\par \par \uc2 "In their efforts to reduce outflows, the authorities have so far avoided contentious, high profile measures such as formally re-imposing restrictions on outflows or re-introducing rules on the sale of U.S. dollar receipts by exporters, for fear of damaging the reputation of China's reform process," said Louis Kuijs, head of Asia Economics at Oxford Economics.\par \par \uc2 "Our analysis suggests, however, that they are likely to end up taking such steps eventually."\par \par \uc2 COULD HAVE BEEN WORSE?\par \par \uc2 The drop in January's reserves would have been worse if not for a sudden reversal in the surging U.S. dollar in January, some analysts said. The softer dollar boosted the value of non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that Beijing holds.\par \par \uc2 "Based on our calculation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  valuation effect alone would lead to a sizeable increase of reserves by US$28 billion," economists at Citi said in a note.\par \par \uc2 However, despite tighter capital curbs and a bounce in the yuan, Citi estimated net capital outflows still intensified to nearly $71 billion in January from $51 billion in December.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the pressure, many Chinese may have exchanged yuan for dollars and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to travel overseas during the long Lunar New Year holidays.\par \par \uc2 "Today's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve number suggests that the authorities are willing to trade a relatively stable yuan-dollar exchange rate for falling \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves because of financial stability concerns," the economists at Citi added.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has gained nearly 1 percent against the dollar so far this year.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists polled by Reuters expect it will resume its descent soon, falling to near-decade lows, especially if the U.S. continues to raise interest rates, which would trigger fresh capital outflows from emerging economies such as China and test Beijing's enhanced capital controls.\par \par \uc2 The drop in reserves in January was mainly due to \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it sold foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bought yuan, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator, the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE), said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 But SAFE said that changes in China's reserves were normal and the market should not pay too much attention to the $3 trillion level.\par \par \uc2 HOW LOW CAN THEY GO?\par \par \uc2 While estimates vary widely, some analysts believe China needs to retain a minimum of $2.6 trillion to $2.8 trillion under the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's (\uc2 IMF\uc2 's) adequacy measures.\par \par \uc2 If the dollar's rally gets back on track, fears of a yuan devaluation would likely spark more intense capital flight.\par \par \uc2 "The fact that China holds less than $3 trillion in reserves right now means that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to rethink its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy," said Zhou Hao, a senior emerging markets economist at Commerzbank in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 It does not make much sense to keep sharply draining reserves if market expectations of further yuan weakness are unlikely to change, he added.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Beijing Monitoring Desk and Kevin Yao; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-2-7T13:54:48.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170207ed2700g2b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{