\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,552 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2017\par 02:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0159 GMT - Japan's 2017 trade surplus will likely top last year's Y4 trillion, considering brightening export prospects, but there are considerable uncertainties over what would happen next because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , says SMBC Nikko Securities economist Yoshimasa Maruyama. "There are considerable risks that he will try to steer his policy to lessen the US' trade deficit with Japan based on his 'America First' philosophy," Maruyama says. That would hurt Japan's exports directly as well as indirectly by undermining global growth and by impacting US trade with China and Mexico, where many Japanese firms make export products, he adds. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Smoothing out bumps in the data shows that Australian underlying inflation has been running at an annualized pace of 1.7% over the past two quarters. This looks like a fair approximation of the current inflation pulse, says CBA. It is consistent with the tepid pace of wages growth. It does, however, look like the slowdown in inflation has come to an end a development that supports a market pricing view that 1.5% is the low point for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's cash rate. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT - The AUD/USD pair took the softer-than-expected Australian 4Q inflation data within its stride. Whilst it did briefly touch an intraday 3-session low, it remains relatively close to 11-week highs after an impressive start to the year, says Matt Simpson, senior market analyst at ThinkMarkets. Momentum favors a buy-the-dip approach, he adds. Momentum continues to favor another attempt to test 0.7600 and, unless the dollar can muster up some strength, he expects the Aussie dollar to remain above 0.7500. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The yield on the benchmark 10-year Japanese government bond briefly hits a one month high of 0.080% before edging down to 0.075%, with investors selling the relative safety of Japanese government debt as risk-taking sentiment improves. Tokyo stocks are higher with the benchmark up 1.3%. The yields on the newest 20-, 40-year JGBs also inch up, briefly touching 11-month highs of 0.655% and 0.995%, respectively. Lead JGB futures are 0.40 lower at 149.99. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT - China's bond market is likely to face pressure after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates on a key liquidity facility late Tuesday. "While the size of the rate increase is modest, the signal seems clear that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has stepped up the tightening bias," said \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  in a note. The yield on China's benchmark 10-year government bond was last at 3.32% compared with an intraday high of 3.4% in mid-December, and 10-year government bond futures are down 0.8%. Late Tuesday, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  raised the rate on medium-term lending facility liquidity injections by 10 basis points, to 2.95% for the six-month and 3.10% for the one-year tenor. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has been seesawing between tightening and easing measures for the past several months as officials balance opposing aims of deleveraging and market stability. The pressures came to a head in December, when steeper borrowing costs, a more hawkish Fed and default scares pushed the yield on China's government bonds to a 15-month high. (rachel.rosethal@wsj.com; @rosenthalrc)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Taiwan's accelerating economy is set to run out of steam soon, says Gareth Leather, senior Asia economist at Capital Economics. He cites weak growth and structural changes in China, the risk of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's administration crimping trade, feeble demand at home and little chance of policy support from Taipei. "China's move up the value chain...has led to Chinese companies producing more of the high-value semi-conductors and other intermediate goods that were previously imported from Taiwan," he says. Domestic demand is also likely to weaken, given a downturn in the property sector, and higher oil prices that will eat into consumers' real income, he adds. "Having more than doubled over the previous seven years, property prices are now falling. Consumer spending has generally suffered on past occasions when property prices have dropped," he says. Capital sees 2017 growth at 1.5%, well below a government forecast of 1.87%. (paul.jackson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The rebound in Australia's headline inflation in the fourth quarter of last year has been baked in the cake for some time, so the real news is that underlying inflation did not budge up, says \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . If this remains a theme for 2017, as we expect, then the RBA may yet cut interest rates from 1.5% now to 1.0%. Underlying inflation has remained well below the RBA's 2-3% target. Yes, an average of the trimmed mean and weighted median measures rose from 1.5% to 1.6%. It remains in line with the RBA's forecasts. This means the RBA won't be dusting off the interest rate cut button in February, CE added. But if underlying inflation rises slower than the RBA expects, then rates may be cut to 1.0% later this year. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT - Amid the Australian dollar's post-CPI slide, "the market was certainly gunning for the topside," says Stephen Innes, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at \uc2 OANDA\uc2 . He adds, "Maybe this is just a temporary blip," with the move possibly being more about short-term position capitulation and a subtle repricing lower. "However, given the recent uptick in regional inflation and surging commodity market, I expect today's selloff to be limited." The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen from around US$0.7598 to US$0.7537 on the report. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT - \uc2 Vedanta Resources\uc2  (VED.LN) prices its US$1 billion 5.5-year US dollar bonds overnight, helping to refinance part of its bonds maturing in 2018 and 2019 and extending its average debt maturity. The newly-priced bonds carry a coupon of 6.375%, which was 37.5 basis points tighter than the initial yield guidance. Research firm Lucror Analytics says its credit bias on the Indian oil and metals firm remains "positive" and the proposed merger with \uc2 Cairn India\uc2  (532792.BY), if completed, will further enhance its credit profile. For those who want more exposure to Vedanta, Lucror says the company's newly-priced bonds represent a great opportunity and expects bond yield to trade tighter. "Technicals should still be positive with the recovery of commodities prices and potential headline support from positive rating actions," says Lucror. (carol.chan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT - Whether President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  likes it or not, Japan's exports to the US are more likely to keep recovering than to falter, says Meiji Yasuda Life Insurance economist Yuichi Kodama. "The US economy is bouncing back quite strongly," which offsets structural obstacles to Japan's US-bound exports, such as Japanese firms' reluctance to cut export prices, Kodama says. Data released Wednesday show Japan's exports to the US gain 1.3% in value terms in December, the first gain in 10 months. In volume terms they rose 5.2%, in the second straight month of expansion. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT - \uc2 The Bank of Korea\uc2 's scope for more easing is narrowing, due to uncertainty over impeached President Park Geun-hye and a possible delay in reforming industrial structures, say economists at \uc2 Australia and New Zealand Banking Group\uc2 . "Downside risks to growth...suggest there is still room for another 25 (basis point) rate cut in the near term," ANZ says after BOK data showed Korea's GDP growth slowed in 4Q. "However, political uncertainty will likely limit the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's maneuvering space. The introduction of more structural reforms is needed to mitigate the risks surrounding financial stability. In the absence of such reforms, the marginal benefit of an interest rate cut will be limited." ANZ says. The BOK kept its base rate unchanged for a 7th month in January. ANZ has pushed back its call for another rate cut from the first quarter to the end of the second quarter. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT - China sets the yuan 0.4% weaker against the dollar, putting the midpoint at CNY6.8596 versus CNY6.8331 Tuesday after overnight gains for the dollar and onshore trading ending yesterday at CNY6.8570. They're both factor in where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets the yuan in the daily fix. But \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  poses a good question: How will China respond if the dollar pulls back for a sustained period after its dizzying rally? In 1H16, China let the yuan weaken against its basket of trading partners--a time the dollar also broadly moved lower. Policy makers could pursue the same strategy this year, the investment bank says, as doing so would improve trade competitiveness and diminish \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's need to spend \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for "useless \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 24, 2017 20:59 ET (01:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170125ed1p00054\par }\page {\par\f