\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2017\par 02:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Taiwan's accelerating economy is set to run out of steam soon, says Gareth Leather, senior Asia economist at Capital Economics. He cites weak growth and structural changes in China, the risk of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's administration crimping trade, feeble demand at home and little chance of policy support from Taipei. "China's move up the value chain...has led to Chinese companies producing more of the high-value semi-conductors and other intermediate goods that were previously imported from Taiwan," he says. Domestic demand is also likely to weaken, given a downturn in the property sector, and higher oil prices that will eat into consumers' real income, he adds. "Having more than doubled over the previous seven years, property prices are now falling. Consumer spending has generally suffered on past occasions when property prices have dropped," he says. Capital sees 2017 growth at 1.5%, well below a government forecast of 1.87%. (paul.jackson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The rebound in Australia's headline inflation in the fourth quarter of last year has been baked in the cake for some time, so the real news is that underlying inflation did not budge up, says \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . If this remains a theme for 2017, as we expect, then the RBA may yet cut interest rates from 1.5% now to 1.0%. Underlying inflation has remained well below the RBA's 2-3% target. Yes, an average of the trimmed mean and weighted median measures rose from 1.5% to 1.6%. It remains in line with the RBA's forecasts. This means the RBA won't be dusting off the interest rate cut button in February, CE added. But if underlying inflation rises slower than the RBA expects, then rates may be cut to 1.0% later this year. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT - Amid the Australian dollar's post-CPI slide, "the market was certainly gunning for the topside," says Stephen Innes, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at \uc2 OANDA\uc2 . He adds, "Maybe this is just a temporary blip," with the move possibly being more about short-term position capitulation and a subtle repricing lower. "However, given the recent uptick in regional inflation and surging commodity market, I expect today's selloff to be limited." The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen from around US$0.7598 to US$0.7537 on the report. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT - \uc2 Vedanta Resources\uc2  (VED.LN) prices its US$1 billion 5.5-year US dollar bonds overnight, helping to refinance part of its bonds maturing in 2018 and 2019 and extending its average debt maturity. The newly-priced bonds carry a coupon of 6.375%, which was 37.5 basis points tighter than the initial yield guidance. Research firm Lucror Analytics says its credit bias on the Indian oil and metals firm remains "positive" and the proposed merger with \uc2 Cairn India\uc2  (532792.BY), if completed, will further enhance its credit profile. For those who want more exposure to Vedanta, Lucror says the company's newly-priced bonds represent a great opportunity and expects bond yield to trade tighter. "Technicals should still be positive with the recovery of commodities prices and potential headline support from positive rating actions," says Lucror. (carol.chan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT - Whether President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  likes it or not, Japan's exports to the US are more likely to keep recovering than to falter, says Meiji Yasuda Life Insurance economist Yuichi Kodama. "The US economy is bouncing back quite strongly," which offsets structural obstacles to Japan's US-bound exports, such as Japanese firms' reluctance to cut export prices, Kodama says. Data released Wednesday show Japan's exports to the US gain 1.3% in value terms in December, the first gain in 10 months. In volume terms they rose 5.2%, in the second straight month of expansion. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT - \uc2 The Bank of Korea\uc2 's scope for more easing is narrowing, due to uncertainty over impeached President Park Geun-hye and a possible delay in reforming industrial structures, say economists at \uc2 Australia and New Zealand Banking Group\uc2 . "Downside risks to growth...suggest there is still room for another 25 (basis point) rate cut in the near term," ANZ says after BOK data showed Korea's GDP growth slowed in 4Q. "However, political uncertainty will likely limit the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's maneuvering space. The introduction of more structural reforms is needed to mitigate the risks surrounding financial stability. In the absence of such reforms, the marginal benefit of an interest rate cut will be limited." ANZ says. The BOK kept its base rate unchanged for a 7th month in January. ANZ has pushed back its call for another rate cut from the first quarter to the end of the second quarter. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT - China sets the yuan 0.4% weaker against the dollar, putting the midpoint at CNY6.8596 versus CNY6.8331 Tuesday after overnight gains for the dollar and onshore trading ending yesterday at CNY6.8570. They're both factor in where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets the yuan in the daily fix. But \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  poses a good question: How will China respond if the dollar pulls back for a sustained period after its dizzying rally? In 1H16, China let the yuan weaken against its basket of trading partners--a time the dollar also broadly moved lower. Policy makers could pursue the same strategy this year, the investment bank says, as doing so would improve trade competitiveness and diminish \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's need to spend \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for "useless \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 0125 GMT - USD/CHF Intraday: Further upside. The pair recorded a succession of higher tops and higher bottoms since Jan 24 and is trading on the upside. The upward momentum is further reinforced by its rising 20-period and 50-period moving averages. The relative strength index broke above a bearish trend line and is above its neutrality level at 50. As long as 0.9985 is support, look for a further upside toward 1.0020 and even 1.0050 in extension. Only a break below 0.9985 would turn the outlook to negative with a down target at 0.9955. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0124 GMT - GBP/USD Intraday: Caution. The pair is trading above its 20-period and 50-period moving averages, while the 20-period moving average just crossed above the 50-period one. The relative strength index is above its neutrality level at 50 and lacks downward momentum. However, 1.2535(Jan 23 & 24 tops) is playing a key resistance role. We are cautious now. As long as the key level at 1.2535 is not broken, we keep our negative view unchanged with a down target at 1.2490 first. Alternatively, a break above 1.2535 would turn the outlook to positive with an up target at 1.2580. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT - NZD/USD Intraday: Rebound expected. The pair broke below its 20-peirod moving average but is still trading above the rising 50-period one, which plays support role and maintains the upside bias. The relative strength index is above its neutrality level at 50 and lacks downward momentum. Even though a continuation of the consolidation cannot be ruled out, its extent should be limited. As long as 0.7230 is support, look for a further upside toward 0.7270 and even 0.7300 in extension. Only a break below 0.7230 would turn the outlook to negative with a down target at 0.7210. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT - EUR/GBP Intraday: Downside prevails. The pair remains on the downside, and is looking for a lower bottom. The 20-period moving average has just crossed below the 50-period one. And the relative strength index is below 50 and lacks upward momentum. As long as 0.8585 holds as the key resistance, expect a drop to 0.8550. Alternatively, a break above 0.8585 would call for a bounce toward 0.8600 at first. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 24 Jan 2017 20:42 ET  Global \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk -2-\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT - EUR/JPY Intraday: Towards 122.35. The pair remains in a rising trend channel, and is continuing its rebound. Both 20-period and 50-period moving averages should maintain a bullish bias. And the relative strength index has been supported by a rising trend line. As long as 121.90 is not broken down, further bounce is preferred with 122.35 and 12.65 as targets. Alternatively, if the pair breaks below 121.90, a drop toward 121.55 is likely. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 24, 2017 20:42 ET (01:42 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170125ed1p0001x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
