\par\par \b \uc2 China in Bitcoin Crackdown -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 867 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2017\par 08:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B11\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Regulators criticize virtual-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchanges; analysts cite outflow worries \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Chao Deng \f28 \par \par \uc2 Chinese banking regulators said two bitcoin exchanges in Beijing improperly engaged in margin financing and failed to impose controls to prevent money laundering, a development that could hurt trading of the virtual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its biggest market.\par \par \uc2 The action by \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signals heightened government scrutiny of bitcoin trading on the mainland, which has been allowed to expand largely unfettered since 2013.\par \par \uc2 "These exchanges have been in violation by getting into the margin financing business, causing abnormal market volatility," said a statement by the business-management division of the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The inspection also found these exchanges didn't put in proper international anti-money-laundering controls."\par \par \uc2 Margin financing involves lending money to investors so they can increase their bets.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the Shanghai branch of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said a bitcoin exchange there "went beyond its business scope and was in violation by getting into capital services," without elaborating.\par \par \uc2 The statements late Wednesday followed inspections of bitcoin exchanges OKCoin and Huobi that began Jan. 11 in Beijing and of Shanghai bitcoin exchange BTCC that started on Jan. 6.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman at Huobi said "the investigation is ongoing" and declined to comment further. OKCoin couldn't be reached for comment.\par \par \uc2 BTCC Chief Executive Bobby Lee said his firm hasn't been formally notified of any improper activity or directed to make changes. He said BTCC is cooperating with regulators, providing data on the number of users and deposit and withdrawal amounts.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the findings, the bitcoin exchanges could face penalties and be asked to make \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  changes, according to a person familiar with the situation.\par \par \uc2 Bitcoin analysts say the action by Chinese regulators might stem from the government's escalating concerns about capital outflows. China is bitcoin's largest trading market globally.\par \par \uc2 "The Chinese government is worried about bitcoin's capacity to help Chinese citizens get money out of China and about losses to Chinese speculators," said Gil Luria, director of research at Wedbush Securities.\par \par \uc2 "The mention of money laundering," he added, "likely relates to the government's plan to curtail the ability to use bitcoin to get capital out."\par \par \uc2 Traders say they could no longer get access to new margin loans on the bitcoin exchanges after inspections started.\par \par \uc2 While the long-term implications of China's actions are unclear, prices of the virtual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday had fallen about 12% since news of the inspections first broke, and were down about 20% from their high earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 "In the short term, the uncertainty will keep the price depressed," said Neil Woodfine, chief operating officer of Beijing-based Remitsy, which helps businesses make payments to China using bitcoin. But "the most important thing is getting some rules that draw a line in the sand. Once that's in place, the whole industry can grow without fearing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to limit the ability of Chinese individuals and companies to move their wealth abroad, a trend that contributes to a depreciating Chinese yuan and risks destabilizing the broader financial system.\par \par \uc2 A virtual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  created in 2008, bitcoin is a now widely distributed and has a total aggregate value of $14.6 billion. It is used by some people to remit money and make payments.\par \par \uc2 Bitcoin first took off in China more than three years ago, starting with tech-savvy traders who helped pump prices to above $1,000 a coin for the first time. China's three largest exchanges accounted for 94% of global trading volumes in the past two years, according to Bitcoinity, a data-tracker.\par \par \uc2 The buying craze stopped when Chinese authorities declared in December 2013 that banks and payment businesses shouldn't engage in bitcoin transactions. The Chinese returned to buying in 2016 when bitcoin took off again, aided by geopolitical uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 While bitcoin is currently a drop in the bucket when it comes to capital outflows from China, analysts say it has potential to move large amounts of money under the radar of regulators. About $2 billion of bitcoin moved out of China in 2016, estimates \uc2 Chainalysis\uc2 , a New York start-up that tracks movement of bitcoin.\par \par \uc2 Traders say some small Chinese firms are using bitcoin to settle import-export transactions. And individuals use bitcoin to buy items in the virtual world, such as videogames, cloud computing services, and internet VPNs, insiders say.\par \par \uc2 Yu Shu, an employee at HaoBTC, a bitcoin wallet business in Beijing, said he has used bitcoin to buy videogames, a process he said was as easy as using China's ubiquitous Alipay online-payment platform.\par \par \uc2 His colleague, Xiong Tyler Yue, an early Chinese investor in bitcoin, said he used bitcoin to make a roughly 1,000 yuan ($146) donation to Alabama-based free-market think tank Mises Institute.\par \par \uc2 "It's a personal freedom," Mr. Yue said. "Capital is smart and always goes to the area of more opportunities."\par \par \uc2 --Liyan Qi contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Chao Deng at Chao.Deng@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 20, 2017 02:33 ET (07:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170120ed1k000nq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
