\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan firms, academics urge Beijing to loosen grip\b0\par\par\uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 2017\par 06:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, Jan 17 (Reuters) - China's yuan firmed slightly\par against the dollar on Tuesday, buoyed by its firmer offshore\par counterpart and as corporates  eased off heavy dollar purchases\par this week.\par     The onshore market has shown more stability, with short\par sellers shying away from shorting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since the\par authorities engineered a jump in offshore borrowing costs\par earlier this month, but traders say sentiment remains weak on\par views the yuan may soon resume its downward trend.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate \par at 6.8992 per dollar prior to market open, weaker than the\par previous fix 6.8874. \par     The spot market opened at 6.9033 per dollar and\par was changing hands at 6.8971 at midday, 34 pips firmer than the\par previous late session close and only 0.03 percent softer than\par the midpoint.\par     "The market was quite stable and balanced in the morning,"\par said a Shanghai-based trader at a Chinese bank, noting companies\par were waiting for better quotes to purchase the greenback. "A\par stronger offshore yuan and the continued consolidation in the\par U.S. dollar have dampened corporate dollar purchases."\par     Traders were reluctant to sell onshore spot too aggressively\par given stronger offshore rates and expectations that conditions\par could be tight before the week-long Lunar New Year. \par     The spread between onshore and offshore yuan has\par eased to near 400 pips, narrowing from around 1,000 pips in\par early January.\par     As of midday, offshore yuan was trading 0.57 percent firmer\par than the onshore spot at 6.8583 per dollar. \par     The market was also awaiting clearer direction on the yuan,\par ahead of a new U.S. administration in Washington this week and\par with China's economic growth under greater scrutiny. \par     "I believe the market will have a clearer picture at least\par in the post-Lunar New Year holiday, when the U.S.\par President-elect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  has taken office and announced his\par policies," said another trader at a foreign bank, suggesting\par weak sentiment would likely persist till the beginning of\par February.\par     Liquidity usually thins ahead of the Lunar New Year holiday,\par which will see markets shut from Jan. 27 to Feb. 2.\par     China will lower its 2017 economic growth target to around\par 6.5 percent from last year's 6.5-7 percent target range, policy\par sources told Reuters, reinforcing a policy shift from supporting\par growth to pushing reforms to contain debt and housing risks.\par  \par     Uncertainty over the yuan was also exacerbated by a rare\par public debate among academics advocating that the authorities\par push forward a free float for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Yu Yongding, a scholar at the China Academy of Social\par Sciences and former \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adviser, said on Tuesday that\par China should overcome the "irrational fear" of allowing the\par Chinese yuan to float freely. \par     On Monday, Xiao Lisheng, a senior finance researcher with\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chinese Academy of Social Sciences\f28 \uc2 \f2 , wrote in the official\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Securities Journal suggesting the government stop\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, devalue the yuan and\par let it float freely to restore stability. \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold a net 317.8 billion yuan ($46.08\par billion) worth of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in December, continuing\par efforts to support the sliding yuan. \par     The Thomson Reuters/HKEX Global CNH index, which\par tracks the offshore yuan against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a\par daily basis, stood at 96.04, weaker than the previous day's\par 96.13.\par     Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards contracts\par (NDFs), considered the best available proxy for\par forward-looking market expectations of the yuan's value, traded\par at 7.1542, 3.56 percent weaker than the midpoint.\par     One-year NDFs are settled against the midpoint, not the spot\par rate.\par \par     The yuan market at 0359 GMT: \par     \par     ONSHORE SPOT:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint      6.8992   6.8874    -0.17%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.8971   6.9005    0.05%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    -0.03%             \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change YTD                       0.72%\par  Spot change since 2005                20.00%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par     Key indexes:\par      \par  Item            Current     Previous  Change\par                                        \par  Thomson         96.04       96.13     -0.1\par  Reuters/HKEX                          \par  CNH index                             \par  Dollar index    101.39      101.18    0.2\par  \par     \par     \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.8583    0.57%\par         *                        \par  Offshore              7.1542    -3.56%\par  non-deliverable                 \par  forwards                        \par                **                \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par \par ($1 = 6.8960 Chinese yuan renminbi)\par \par     \par  (Reporting by Winni Zhou and John Ruwitch)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-1-17T06:35:25.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170117ed1h008kf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
