\par\par \b \uc2 Scholar says free-floating yuan is nothing to fear - paper\b0\par\par\uc2 379 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 2017\par 03:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Jan 17 (Reuters) - China should overcome the "irrational fear" of allowing the Chinese yuan to float freely, an influential government researcher said on Tuesday, as markets seek clear policies in an increasingly complex financial environment.\par \par \uc2 Yu Yongding, a scholar at the China Academy of Social Sciences and former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser, made the comments amid a growing debate among Chinese economists about whether the yuan should be allowed to trade more freely.\par \par \uc2 With no clear direction from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders have been closely scrutinising the comments of influential academics such as Yu for a sign of any future policy movements.\par \par \uc2 "How the future exchange rate will go depends on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and I have no way of reading their minds, but I want to say that we should overcome the irrational fear of a free-floating yuan," Yu was quoted as saying by the official Shanghai Securities News.\par \par \uc2 Yu's comments notwithstanding, traders said it was unlikely that Chinese authorities would loosen their grip on the exchange rate in the near future, noting that there was no clear consensus on what the equilibrium rate would be, for example.\par \par \uc2 Yu told a forum on Monday that China has a huge trade surplus, $3 trillion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's fastest economic growth, and a strong government.\par \par \uc2 "(China) should be the country least afraid of a fluctuating exchange rate," he was quoted as saying.\par \par \uc2 Yu has also advocated that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and instead preserve its dwindling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 He suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set a "bottom line" of 25 percent for the yuan to depreciate.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell to near six-year lows in December, but held just above the critical $3 trillion level, as authorities stepped in to support the weakening yuan ahead of U.S. President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's inauguration.\par \par \uc2 Though economists insist Beijing has been trying to prop up rather than weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Trump has threatened to declare China to be a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator and slap punitive tariffs on Chinese goods.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 6.8986 yuan) (Reporting by David Stanway and \uc2 Jing Wang\uc2 ; Editing by Eric Meijer) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-1-17T03:45:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170117ed1h003xp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
