\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  should stop  \highlight22\uc2 intervening \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market and let yuan float- researcher\b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2017\par 03:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Jan 16 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, devalue the yuan and let it float freely to restore stability, a senior researcher at a government-backed think tank said.\par \par \uc2 Xiao Lisheng, a finance expert with the \uc2 Chinese Academy of Social Sciences\uc2 , made the remarks in an article on Monday in the official China Securities Journal amid a growing debate among the country's economists on whether authorities should let the closely-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade more freely.\par \par \uc2 The yuan lost 6.6 percent against the dollar last year, the biggest annual loss since 1994.\par \par \uc2 "The more the government delays the release of depreciation pressure, the greater the impact and destructive power of the release of depreciation pressure will be," Xiao wrote.\par \par \uc2 The authorities should "let the yuan exchange rate have a one-off adjustment to realize a free float" of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.\par \par \uc2 The yuan is allowed to trade in a band of 2 percent on either side of a daily reference rate managed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Authorities have said repeatedly there was no basis for continued depreciation of the unit, but many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists predict a further weakening this year if the U.S. dollar remains strong, spurring further capital outflows from China.\par \par \uc2 Xiao said the current mid-point formation mechanism, adopted in 2015, is still immature and in transition, although it has eased depreciation pressure and curbed sharp declines in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 "But any \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate mechanism without a free float cannot fundamentally reach a market clearing (price)," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 The mechanism for setting the daily reference rate was adopted after a one-off devaluation of the yuan in August 2015. It is opaque, but factors in the closing price from a day earlier and the movements of various other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yu Yongding, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser, has also advocated that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help preserve its dwindling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, and suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set a "bottom line" of 25 percent for the yuan to depreciate.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell to near six-year lows in December, but held just above the critical $3 trillion level, as authorities stepped in to support the weakening yuan ahead of U.S. President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's inauguration.\par \par \uc2 For 2016 as a whole, China's reserves fell nearly $320 billion to $3.011 trillion, on top of a record drop of $513 billion in 2015. (Reporting by Winni Zhou and John Ruwitch; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-1-16T03:31:21.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170116ed1g00496\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
