\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  should halt yuan  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 , preserve reserves- ex-PBOC adviser\b0\par\par\uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2017\par 06:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Jan 12 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should set a "bottom line" of 25 percent for the yuan to depreciate, stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, Yu Yongding, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser, was quoted as saying by financial magazine Caixin on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Yu, an influential economist at a top government think tank, the \uc2 Chinese Academy of Social Sciences\uc2 , has been an advocate of a freer float for the yuan, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  currently allows to a fluctuate 2 percent either side of a midpoint it sets before markets open each day.\par \par \uc2 "It is a very bad policy to let the yuan depreciate gradually and slowly towards a 'reasonable and balanced level' through sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Yu, a former member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Monetary Policy Committee, said in remarks reported on Caixin's website.\par \par \uc2 His comments follow a bout sustained pressure from capital outflows that have resulted in the yuan losing 6.5 percent against the dollar in 2016 - the biggest annual drop since 1994.\par \par \uc2 Despite recovering about 0.5 percent this month, the yuan was still hovering near its lowest in 8-1/2 years, trading around 6.9130 per dollar early Thursday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in onshore and offshore markets have helped steady the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but Yu argued such action to stagger the yuan's decline.\par \par \uc2 "Abandoning \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will not only avoid the unnecessary depletion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, but also greatly reduce the need for capital controls to curb capital outflows," Yu said, arguing that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only encouraged yuan-sellers.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves shrank by $320 billion in 2016, with $41 billion spent in December alone, to end the year at $3.011 trillion - near six year lows.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  has repeatedly pledged to keep the yuan basically stable at a "reasonable and balanced level", while reforming the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to make the yuan more flexible.\par \par \uc2 Yu's remarks feed into a debate among Chinese economists whether the authorities should stick to the current strategy or let the yuan float.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Bloomberg News on Monday, PBOC advisor Fan Gang said the decline in China's foreign reserves is good news in the long-run and described the yuan as being at a "turning point" after possibly being overvalued in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Yu said the yuan has yet to reach its "reasonable and balanced level", or equilibrium, with the dollar broadly stronger and confidence in China's economy also a key factor.\par \par \uc2 He said market expectations for the yuan to depreciate further are unlikely to have changed, though the authorities have helped the yuan's recent recovery by making speculation against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prohibitively expensive, and by tightening some capital controls.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should find a "good opportunity" to declare a halt to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and only resume action if the yuan weakens beyond 25 percent, he said, without elaborating.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, Yu said China cannot let the yuan fall more than 25 percent.\par \par \uc2 "If the yuan depreciates too much, China can step up capital controls, we have a large amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, and ultimately, we have a bottom line, we cannot allow the yuan to depreciate more than 25 percent," Yu said according to a transcript posted on Chinese news portal Hexun.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kevin Yao; Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-1-15T05:54:19.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170112ed1c009yd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
