\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ\u8217\'20\'19s Daily Shot: How Will Americans Spend Their Tax-Cut Bounties?\b0\par\par\uc2 1,538 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2017\par 16:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 To receive the Daily Shot newsletter in your inbox, please sign up at our Email Center.\par \par \uc2 Have questions, feedback or comments? Contact author Lev.Borodovsky@DowJones.com.\par \par \uc2 The Daily Shot: 06-Jan-17 \u8226\'20\'22 China \u8226\'20\'22 The United States \u8226\'20\'22 Equity Markets \u8226\'20\'22 Emerging Markets \u8226\'20\'22 Europe \u8226\'20\'22 Australia \u8226\'20\'22 Global Developments \u8226\'20\'22 Commodities \u8226\'20\'22 Credit \u8226\'20\'22 Food for Thought\par \par \uc2 1. Beijing's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yuan continued to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher on Thursday. The charts below show the 1-month USD/CNY forwards as well as the offshore yuan (USD/CNH). This move had a significant impact on global markets, sending the dollar sharply lower against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (more on this shortly).\par \par \uc2 Some have suggested that suddenly strengthening the renminbi may be China's preparation for the upcoming "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator" battle with the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 2. Part of the reason for this unusually sharp rally in the offshore yuan is another liquidity squeeze in Hong Kong. Here is the overnight interbank rate.\par \par \uc2 3. After approaching record highs, Bitcoin suddenly tumbled by over 20% before recovering some ground. Bitcoin specialists point to China strengthening the yuan as being the primary reason for the cryptocurrency's crash.\par \par \uc2 Source: @WSJ; Read full article\par \par \uc2 4. In other developments, here is an updated debt-to-GDP ratio for China, which continued to climb in 2016. Will public debt levels begin to rise going forward to support economic growth?\par \par \uc2 Source: Macquarie\par \par \uc2 5. It's worth pointing out that China's labor productivity growth remains on a downward trend. With the nation's population growth slowing, maintaining a 6-7% output growth will be difficult without further stimulus from the public sector.\par \par \uc2 Source: Macquarie\par \par \uc2 6. The recent recovery in industrial output has increased air pollution again, despite Beijing's efforts to tackle the problem.\par \par \uc2 Source: @ECONdailycharts, @Tmp_Research; Read full article\par \par \uc2 1. The dollar tumbled in response to \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the renminbi. The uncertainty mentioned in the FOMC minutes as well the ADP private payrolls miss - which pushed Treasury yields sharply lower - contributed to the decline as well.\par \par \uc2 2. Treasuries rallied, with the biggest one-day 10yr yield decline we've seen in months.\par \par \uc2 3. The ADP private nonfarm payrolls missed forecasts by a significant margin. While most analysts would view this as weakness in demand (fewer job openings are created), some have suggested that it could be a supply issue (not enough qualified workers are available). Whatever the case, this result does not bode well for the official payrolls report Friday morning (175k expected).\par \par \uc2 The second chart below shows the payrolls breakdown by sector in November and December.\par \par \uc2 4. The non-manufacturing sector activity report from the ISM, on the other hand, beat forecasts. New orders were especially healthy. It's worth noting that even this upbeat report pointed to softer job growth in the service sector.\par \par \uc2 The same ISM report also showed the non-manufacturing price gains grinding higher.\par \par \uc2 As an aside, investors are paying attention to these inflation figures. The chart below shows shares outstanding in the largest TIPS ETF.\par \par \uc2 5. Where will Americans spend their extra cash if the Trump administration and Congress manage to cut personal taxes? The chart below shows products/services that are most sensitive to extra disposable income.\par \par \uc2 Source: \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , @MattGarrett3\par \par \uc2 Note that it's durable goods that will benefit the most, albeit with some lag.\par \par \uc2 Source: \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , @MattGarrett3\par \par \uc2 5. Speaking of softer hiring trends, are we looking at a retail recession in the US?\par \par \uc2 Source: Business Insider, @jessefelder; Read full article\par \par \uc2 1. Staying on the topic of retail, the sector underperformance is back as \uc2 Macy's\uc2  and \uc2 Kohl's\uc2  take a hit.\par \par \uc2 Source: @WSJ; Read full article\par \par \uc2 2. Amazon continues to dominate the holiday season - some ten times the size of the next biggest seller.\par \par \uc2 Source: @carlquintanilla, @shaig\par \par \uc2 3. Analysts are predicting record earnings per share this year for the S&P 500 companies. However, as the chart below shows, analysts have generally been too optimistic.\par \par \uc2 Source: @FactSet; Read full article\par \par \uc2 4. Next, we have a chart of the S&P500 and copper prices since the elections. Should we ignore the divergence?\par \par \uc2 Source: Bloomberg\par \par \uc2 5. Here are the 20 most active ETFs (dollar volume) in 2016. Note that three of these are volatility derivatives. There has been a great deal of interest in shorting volatility last year, especially with complacency setting in.\par \par \uc2 Source: Citi, @NickatFP\par \par \uc2 6. Implied market correlation continues to fall due to continuing outperformance of sectors that are expected to benefit from policy changes in Washington.\par \par \uc2 1. \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tried to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to halt the peso's free-fall. However, Trump's tweet about Toyota's investment in Mexico negated much of the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 's efforts.\par \par \uc2 Source: Bloomberg.com; Read full article\par \par \uc2 The country's bonds are still getting clobbered - here is the 10yr yield.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting that the US media seems to be ignoring the country's mounting troubles. Mexico is the second-largest export market for the US (third if one combines all the \uc2 EU\uc2  countries). Moreover, instability and rising poverty in Mexico creates a major security problem for the US. For example, heroin coming to the US will become much cheaper as the peso falls.\par \par \uc2 2. Brazil's industrial production came in below expectations as the recovery remains tepid.\par \par \uc2 3. Nigeria's economy continues to struggle despite higher oil prices. Here is a summary from World Economics.\par \par \uc2 Source: World Economics\par \par \uc2 The nation's government bond yields are back above 16% - which is unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 4. Foreigners are taking another look at the Philippines, with inflows driving domestic stock and bond prices higher.\par \par \uc2 5. The Russian ruble is having a great start of the year, with the dollar now buying less than 60 rubles.\par \par \uc2 1. Portuguese bonds remain under pressure, with the nation expected to issue quite a bit of debt early in the year. Here is the Portugal - Germany 10yr spread.\par \par \uc2 2. The Eurozone finally pulls out of the producer price deflation that started in 2013.\par \par \uc2 3. The UK's service sector keeps surprising to the upside, with the latest PMI figure beating expectations by a considerable margin.\par \par \uc2 The nation's exports jumped by most since 2011 on surging coal and iron ore prices.\par \par \uc2 1. Continuing with the topic of exports, here is \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's leading indicator for global trade.\par \par \uc2 Source: \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , @MattGarrett3\par \par \uc2 The next chart from \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  shows the export orders component of the manufacturing PMI.\par \par \uc2 Source: \uc2 Credit Suisse\par \par \uc2 2. The drag from global reductions in inventories seems to be ending. We saw that with the pickup in US inventories in November.\par \par \uc2 Source: \uc2 Credit Suisse\par \par \uc2 3. Here is an estimate of how much the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are overvalued/undervalued relative to the US dollar.\par \par \uc2 Source: World Economics\par \par \uc2 4. Finally, the chart below shows the returns and the Sharpe ratio for major asset classes in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and in dollars.\par \par \uc2 Source: \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , @MattGarrett3\par \par \uc2 1. Gold and most other commodities rallied in response to the drop in the US dollar.\par \par \uc2 2. Measured in terms of days of supply, US crude oil inventories are closely following last year's path.\par \par \uc2 1. US leveraged loan inflows remain healthy.\par \par \uc2 Source: @lcdnews\par \par \uc2 2. US 3-month LIBOR hits 1% for the first time in years. Once again, it's worth pointing out that the coupon in most corporate loans (such as the ones in the chart above) is based on LIBOR. Things will go pear shaped if (as currently proposed by the Republicans) interest expense can no longer be deducted from income for tax purposes.\par \par \uc2 1. Let's begin the Food for Thought section with the US federal prison population by administration.\par \par \uc2 Source: @FactTank, @Tmp_Research; Read full article\par \par \uc2 Source: @FactTank, @Tmp_Research; Read full article\par \par \uc2 2. Americans are putting more into their 401k.\par \par \uc2 Source: @business, @Tmp_Research; Read full article\par \par \uc2 3. Here is the updated US population growth. Without a smart immigration policy, this trend will cap US economic expansion going forward (given that the productivity growth remains subpar). Hopefully, US politicians pay attention to what's going on in Japan.\par \par \uc2 Source: @ftdata; Read full article\par \par \uc2 4. Where are the all the \uc2 UN\uc2  troops (100k personnel under the organization's control)?\par \par \uc2 Source: @washingtonpost, @PostGraphics, @Tmp_Research; Read full article\par \par \uc2 5. The majority of residents of developed economies believe we are close to world war.\par \par \uc2 Source: @YouGov, @Tmp_Research; Read full article\par \par \uc2 6. Future job gains in the US are expected to benefit women, hurt men.\par \par \uc2 Source: @paul1kirby, @nytimes, @Tmp_Research; Read full article\par \par \uc2 7. Who owns Trump's debt?\par \par \uc2 Source: @WSJ; Read full article\par \par \uc2 8. Support for the CIA by party.\par \par \uc2 Source: @CarrieNBCNews, @Tmp_Research; Read full article\par \par \uc2 If you haven't already done so, please take a few moments to complete our survey on your prediction for the economy and the markets in 2017. The results will be published here in a few days.\par \par \uc2 Have a great weekend!\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 06, 2017 10:05 ET (15:05 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170106ed16001xx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
