\par\par \b \uc2 The Best and Worst Asia Calls of 2016 -- Heard on the Street\b0\par\par\uc2 1,022 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2016\par 14:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Every day, Heard on the Street makes calls on markets, corporate strategy and economy policy. The writers -- based in New York, London, Hong Kong and San Francisco -- try to explain what is going on and provide useful advice for readers.\par \par \uc2 The columns are deeply reported with a point of view. Sometimes they are prescient, sometimes wrong, but should always be well argued. Today, we take an Asia-focused look back through the 1,247 pieces the writers produced in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Chinese Commodities - Nathaniel Taplin\par \par \uc2 One of our best calls this year was for lower Asian coal prices, with benchmark Newcastle thermal now down around 30% from its mid-November peak. Our Nov. 8 column highlighted the risks to Australian coal from the elimination of Chinese coal transport bottleneck -- a key support for imports in the past -- and the peaking credit cycle in China. Shares of major thermal coal producers like \uc2 Glencore PLC\uc2  have been punished as coal benchmarks have dropped -- the firm's shares are now down close to 10% from their early December peak.\par \par \uc2 Another key call was for higher rates in China. At a time when China still seemed relatively insulated from "the Trump trade," our Nov. 28 column raised doubts about whether \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be able to halt rising benchmark yields given rebounding inflation and the pressure on the yuan. The Chinese bond market cracked after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raised rates\par \par \uc2 Financial markets also have begun pricing in the risk to buoyant industrial metals from slowing momentum in China's real-estate market, which we highlighted here. Copper prices, which rose 30% from late October to late November, have now given up around one-third of those gains.\par \par \uc2 Less good was our call on Sinopec, following reports that the firm might reboot an IPO of its marketing business. The firm's shares have traded sideways since midmonth after a brief bounce following the news proved short-lived.\par \par \uc2 Chinese Banks - Anjani Trivedi\par \par \uc2 Heard has been bearish on Chinese banks since they released first-quarter earnings. The banks were stuck as they tried to maintain a fine balance between profit growth, regulatory capital buffers and net interest margins. Part of the profits have been made up by aggressive off-balance-sheet lending activity and sales of leveraged investment products -- largely made up of bonds -- and facilitated by short-term lending.\par \par \uc2 With its banks increasingly looking under capital pressure, Beijing took to clamping down on bad and hidden loans, and bringing credit risk back on bank balance sheets while simultaneously issuing debt to bolster capital.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, Beijing's balancing act and challenges with its financial system have come to the fore. Pressure points have been exposed in its short-term rate and bond markets as authorities shift into risk-control mode. Bank stocks are up 7.8% since March, but the latest episode of tight liquidity and interbank stress in China's banking system has pushed the stocks of the largest of banks down on average, over 5% in the past month as credit and funding concerns rise.\par \par \uc2 Heard's prediction of a stronger Japanese yen and monetary policy conundrum was a little more straightforward, quickly upended by the Trump-trade and giving the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  some room to breathe.\par \par \uc2 Stocks - Jacky Wong and Abheek Bhattacharya\par \par \uc2 Heard's bearish calls on bubble stocks listed in Hong Kong this year saved investors from big losses. The best call came from a March 11 column, "Why a Shadow Hangs Over This Hong Kong Lighting Stock," on a LED light maker called \uc2 Tech Pro Technology Development\uc2 . The bearish stance was reiterated in a June 8 column titled "Time to Cut Power to This Hong Kong Lighting Play." The stock crashed in July after another short seller Glaucus Research published a report. Tech Pro now trades 92% lower than the March 11 level.\par \par \uc2 Heard has repeatedly cast doubts on \uc2 China Evergrande Group\uc2 , the country's biggest developer, about its debt-led acquisitions, involvement in stock market speculations and aggressive financial engineering. The stock is down 30%. Another overvalued developer, Fullshare Holdings Ltd., has lost 25% of its value since a Sept. 26 column titled "Another $10 Billion Hong Kong Stock-Market Mystery."\par \par \uc2 Some other shorts haven't worked out so far. China Huishan Dairy Holdings Co. is only down 3% since the Jan. 21 column "No More Milk in This Chinese Cow Company" was published. Short seller Muddy Waters published two reports in December against the company. Property developer Zall Group Ltd., which has a cross-holding structure with Fullshare, is little changed since the Nov. 29 column "Two Stock Bubbles, One Ready to Prick."\par \par \uc2 China wasn't the only place the Heard made a good negative call: Shares of \uc2 Toshiba Corp\uc2 . fell 20% after it warned of write-downs in its nuclear business. In August, the Heard said impairments are possible in that business.\par \par \uc2 China-U.S. Relations - Alex Frangos\par \par \uc2 Heard told readers at the turn of the year that pressure on the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue in 2016, but it would come in small doses. True enough, there was no one-off devaluation as some China bears predicted, even if markets did have a rough start to the year. Instead, it was a slow drip of weakness as \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in markets to prop up the yuan against a surging dollar.\par \par \uc2 Soon after the U.S. election, Heard warned that the incoming Trump administration could bring unsettling times for Sino-American economic relations. "If his track record as a businessman is a guide, he is likely to test China with an unorthodox opening move." We didn't think it would happen before he even got to the Oval Office, but our prediction of Mr. Trump's unpredictability came true when he dispensed with decades of diplomatic formality over the status of Taiwan. Markets didn't react as poorly to the fracas as we predicted, but we maintain they may still.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 29, 2016 08:00 ET (13:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161229ecct000yp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
