\par\par \b \uc2 Heard on the Street: China's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Drops but the Pressure Keeps Building -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Frangos \par \uc2 546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2016\par 08:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B12\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 What was notable about China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2016 wasn't that it had its worst year against the dollar in modern economic times. It was the costs expended to make sure it didn't fall even more.\par \par \uc2 The yuan dropped 7% against the dollar this year. That is nearly double the drop from the year before, taking the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a round trip to levels last seen before the global financial crisis. But in the grand scheme of things, the slide did little to relieve pressure on China's financial system.\par \par \uc2 Unlike other emerging markets that have mostly free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as Russia and Brazil, China hasn't had a chance to find its bottom. Chinese investors, therefore, act as if more depreciation is coming, sending money overseas.\par \par \uc2 Those outflows continue to dog the economy. And Beijing continues to fight mightily against them. Through the third quarter this year, China expended $300 billion in reserves on a balance of payments basis \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prevent the yuan from falling even further, more than it spent the previous year over the same period.\par \par \uc2 China still has a bit more than $3 trillion in reserves, which is robust on traditional measures of reserve adequacy, such as import cover. Chinese borrowers continued to pay down foreign debts in 2016, reducing the already unlikely possibility of a foreign debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 Yet another measure of China's robustness continues to erode, showing the cost of all that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ratio of broad money, as measured by M2, to reserves, rose to 7.4 from 6.3 at the end of 2015. Reserves serve as assets on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet, supporting money creation in the economy. Sharp rises in this ratio can be a precursor to a crisis in emerging economies. But even if that isn't the case, it is revealing of the limits to China's financial alchemy.\par \par \uc2 China has long relied on capital inflows to boost the money supply. When capital flows reverse, as has been the case the past few years, the domestic banking system feels the strain. One sign of that is that banks are increasingly reliant on short-term funding in interbank markets, rather than more stable deposits.\par \par \uc2 Keeping those short-term funding markets flush is a tricky task. When \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, it sucks liquidity out of the financial system. It tries to replace that cash by lending money cheaply to banks. But it also leaves the system generally more vulnerable when pressure mounts, as seen by the dislocation in China's bond markets during the dollar's surge in December.\par \par \uc2 The wild card in all of this is geopolitics. President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  moved quickly to confront China after getting elected. An escalation of tensions could change China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  calculus.\par \par \uc2 Politics aside, markets got used to a sliding yuan in 2016. That doesn't mean the warning signals the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sending about China's financial system have gone away.\par \par \uc2 Write to Alex Frangos at alex.frangos@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 29, 2016 02:33 ET (07:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161229ecct000fz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
