\par\par \b \uc2 China Sets Stability of Housing, Financial Sectors as 2017 Priorities\b0\par\par\uc2 922 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2016\par 14:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BEIJING\u8211\'20\'13China\u8217\'20\'19s leaders who just a year ago vowed to tackle chronic economic ills set \u8220\'20\'1Cstability\u8221\'20\'1D as their main goal for next year, pledging to contain asset bubbles and financial risks.\par \par \uc2 At a closed-door Communist Party conclave that ended Friday, senior party officials led by President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  hammered out an economic blueprint for 2017 that emphasizes preventing troubles in the country\u8217\'20\'19s housing market and the increasingly convoluted financial system from spinning out of control.\par \par \uc2 The three-day Central Economic Work Conference was held as concerns are mounting within China\u8217\'20\'19s official circles over how Beijing can effectively deal with a potentially combative Trump administration. Mr. Xi highlighted the economic challenges posed by the rest of the world, according to an official with knowledge of the gathering, though the official left unclear whether Mr. Xi referred to pledges by the U.S. president-elect to take China to task on trade. The potential for a faster pace of interest-rate increases by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  also complicates China\u8217\'20\'19s economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s blueprint marked a contrast to the year-ago goal of fixing problems such as industrial overcapacity and high debt levels, deemed necessary for healthier growth in the long run. The new plan indicates the government will still press ahead with such reforms, but that they have taken a back seat to steadying the economy, according to Chinese officials and economists.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe top priority is on maintaining growth while controlling risks,\u8221\'20\'1D said a senior government adviser. \u8220\'20\'1CThe promised restructuring of the economy will be carried out, but only when overall stability is ensured.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The shift in focus comes as senior officials gear up for a reorganization of the top echelon of the party late next year. As the reshuffle looms, policy makers are trying to keep troubles at bay, with efforts that run counter to pledges to liberalize the economy\u8212\'20\'14such as fresh capital controls.\par \par \uc2 The plan for 2017 also shows China\u8217\'20\'19s economic mandarins are under mounting pressure to keep the country\u8217\'20\'19s economy humming at a rate seen as acceptable to the leadership. Despite expectations that the economy will continue to face headwinds, many economists and government advisers expect leaders to shy away from a gross-domestic-product target for next year below 6.5%\u8212\'20\'14a rate that Mr. Xi has said is needed to realize his goal to double 2010 China\u8217\'20\'19s economy and per capita income by 2020.\par \par \uc2 Actions by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  since his election have added to uncertainty for Beijing, starting withhis protocol-breaking phone conversation with the president of Taiwan in early December, which occurred just after Chinese leaders including Mr. Xi warmly received former U.S. Secretary of State Henry Kissinger in Beijing. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a sense of embarrassment\u8221\'20\'1D among many Chinese officials, one of them said. Another official said it would be a big challenge for Beijing to get Mr. Trump \u8220\'20\'1Cback on track.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While Mr. Trump, who has threatened to label China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator and slap blanket tariffs on Chinese imports, has yet to act on his promises, the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s rate increase this week threatens to flush more money out of China, exacerbating pressure on the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken. In late afternoon mainland trading Friday, one dollar bought 6.9560 yuan, the weakest level for the yuan in 8\u189\'00\'BD years. Many analysts expect the yuan to fall further next year, to between 7.20 and 7.50 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The 2017 economic plan signaled the government\u8217\'20\'19s greater tolerance of a cheaper yuan. In fact, many economists and analysts say, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has little choice as heavy market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would mean draining more yuan out of the financial system.\par \par \uc2 That prospect bodes ill for Chinese manufacturers and private businesses still in need of cash. On the other hand, \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reluctant to cut interest rates or take other aggressive measures to ease credit as that would further fuel bubbles in the country\u8217\'20\'19s bond and property markets\u8212\'20\'14and weaken the yuan further.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the plan for 2017 indicates a tightening of China\u8217\'20\'19s monetary stance, saying the nation\u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy will be \u8220\'20\'1Cprudent and neutral\u8221\'20\'1D as opposed to the \u8220\'20\'1Caccommodative\u8221\'20\'1D stance in previous statements.\par \par \uc2 One chief risk comes from the all-important housing market, which, along with related industries such as construction, accounted for almost a quarter of China\u8217\'20\'19s GDP at the height of the country\u8217\'20\'19s property boom. In recent months, authorities have tightened restrictions on home purchases to rein in a housing frenzy. The leadership stated in the economic plan that \u8220\'20\'1Chouses are for living, not for speculation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But many economists don\u8217\'20\'19t expect that measures taken so far present a long-term solution. Many property developers, meanwhile, are complaining that they are being forced not to increase prices on properties built on land they had paid a high price for, potentially making it harder for them to repay debt.\par \par \uc2 In the southern Chinese city of Huizhou, local authorities last month banned developers from raising prices within two months of putting properties on the market, in a bid to prevent prices from soaring too fast. \u8220\'20\'1CIn the past, it was common for developers to jack up prices within even just a day,\u8221\'20\'1D a local economic-planning official said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t do that anymore now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 16, 2016 08:45 ET (13:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161216eccg001pp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
