\par\par \b \uc2 Global Equities Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 3,021 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2016\par 05:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0455 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] RHB Research says \uc2 Ascendas Real Estate Investment Trust\uc2  (A17U.SG) is its top pick in Singapore's property space, as the trust is a good proxy to the favorable business parks segment. Business parks are better off than residential and retail-focused real estate investment trusts, due mainly to the demand-supply balance and positive rental outlook in the segment. RHB says Ascendas REIT's recent earnings-accretive acquisition of Singapore commercial properties reflects its ability to grow amid a challenging industrial market. "We are also positive on its strategic moves of divesting its China properties and increasing Australia exposure," it notes, keeping its buy call on the stock with S$2.65 target price. A-REIT, which was up in last five sessions, is down 0.4% at S$2.39 while the local benchmark is 0.5% lower. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; Twitter: @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0454 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Could China's ChiNext Price Index, the scene of many a market bloodbath during last year's furious sell-off in the A-share market, be at it again? Monday's 3.1% sell-off in the morning session puts it on track to be the biggest since July 27. That compares to a 2% drop in the Shanghai Composite and a 1.1% dip in Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index. The ChiNext, a growth board on the \uc2 Shenzhen Stock Exchange\uc2  where many fast-growing Chinese stocks are listed and is sometimes referred to as the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  with Chinese characteristics, frequently fell and rose by around 7-8% during last summer's market crash, but has been more subdued recently. In the meantime, levels of margin lending across the Shenzhen and Shanghai markets have recently crept back up to 961 billion yuan, levels not seen since the tail-end of the most recent A-share crash in January.(gregor.hunter@wsj.com; Twitter: @gregorhunter)\par \par \uc2 0427 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shanghai stocks tumbled the most in six months after regulators banned an insurer from buying stocks. The Shanghai Composite Index slumped 2% Monday, as nearly 1,000 stocks retreat. Last Friday, the insurance regulator banned the insurance unit of China's largest property developer, China Evergrande (3333.HK), from buying stocks, citing rules violations. Later Friday, Foresea Life, the arm of Chinese financial conglomerate \uc2 Baoneng Group\uc2  pledged to cut its holdings in Gree, a Shenzhen-listed home-appliance heavyweight stock. This would see it unwind stake-building that drew harsh disapproval from Chinese regulators. "It's clear that the regulator is strongly against such stake buying by insurers that may disturb management of listed firms," said Deng Wenyuan, an analyst at Soochow Securities, "Investors did not expect a sudden tightening up over insurers." (yifan.xie@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0417 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China's domestic shares, called A-shares, are sharply down Monday. The Shanghai Composite slides 2% and the ChiNext-a start-up board in Shenzhen-tumbles 3.1%. Will Leung, head of investment strategy, North East Asia, for \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2 's wealth management division, says the selling is related to a batch of concerns, seemingly unrelated. He points to an ongoing crackdown on share trading by Chinese insurers, a wobbly property sector outlook, and continued drains on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. (jasper.moiseiwitsch@wsj.com; Twitter: @moiseiwitsch)\par \par \uc2 0412 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] OCBC is neutral on Singapore-listed plantations stocks as it expects crude palm oil prices to correct in next one year. The broker attributes this to adequate supply and uncertainty over demand for the commodity. So far this year palm oil prices have recorded strong gains on declining production and tightening inventories. "The consensus view expects crude palm oil production to rebound next year and, at this juncture, weather conditions are forecasted to be supportive for harvesting," it says. OCBC forecasts that crude palm oil price may correct to MYR2,650 a ton in the fourth quarter of 2017 after hovering at MYR2,700-MYR2,900 a ton in the early part of next year. It keeps hold call on Golden Agri (E5H.SG) and \uc2 Wilmar International\uc2  (F34.SG) with a fair value of S$0.37 and S$3.18, respectively. The stocks are up 2.4% and 0.8% at S$0.435 and S$3.64, respectively while the benchmark index is down 0.4%. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; Twitter: @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT -- Health IT firm \uc2 Orion Health\uc2  (OHE.NZ) is up 11%, continuing to rebound from a selloff last week on concerns about its increasing cash burn. "What we did see a couple of days ago was a large cross-selling through the market which I believe has taken the selling pressure off the stock and therefore the bargain hunters have come back into the stock," says Grant Williamson, investment advisor at Hamilton Hindin Greene. "But obviously it has been savaged downward and is still well down year-on-year." (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT -- A substantial lift in \uc2 Morningstar\uc2 's iron-ore forecast for the next two years prompts a substantial lift in the research firm's valuation of Australian producer Fortescue Metals Group Ltd. (FMG.AU). "Fortescue is a single commodity producer with debt and is highly leveraged to the higher forecasts," says \uc2 Morningstar\uc2 , as it hikes its 2017 and 2018 iron ore projections to US$60/ton and US$50/ton, respectively. It had previously forecast a price of US$35 a ton for those years. \uc2 Morningstar\uc2  raises its fair-value estimate on Fortescue, to A$2.80/share from A$1.70. Still, its valuation is well below Fortescue's current price of A$6.81/share. \uc2 Morningstar\uc2 , which has a Sell rating on the stock, says Fortescue still has too much debt and "is vulnerable to cyclical weakness." (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle)\par \par \uc2 0001 GMT -- Better iron-ore, coal and copper price forecasts lead \uc2 Morningstar\uc2  to upgrade its fair-value estimate on \uc2 BHP Billiton Ltd\uc2 . (BHP.AU), but only to A$18.50/share from A$17.00 previously. At A$26.18/share, BHP "remains overvalued," says \uc2 Morningstar\uc2 , as the market bets higher commodity prices will be sustained in the face of structural change in China's economy. "We still see risks to the downside," the research firm says, given commodity prices "are well above the marginal cost of production, particularly for metallurgical coal and copper." (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle)\par \par \uc2 2329 GMT -- Valuations for Australia's biggest banks have, in absolute terms, risen to 13 times expected earnings, which is about 5% above the five-year average, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  estimates. However, the brokerage says the banks on average are trading at about a 3% discount on a relative basis against the All industrials index, excluding banks. It notes \uc2 Commonwealth Bank\uc2  (CBA.AU) is trading at the largest discount to its historical price-earnings valuation, at 5% below the five-year average, while ANZ (ANZ.AU) is now in line with its five-year average. The dispersion between the highest and lowest valuations of the major banks now sits at 2.1 times, with is about 16% below the five-year average, Deutsche says. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 2324 GMT -- The fortunes of \uc2 BHP Billiton Ltd\uc2 .'s (BHP.AU) oil business have taken a positive turn. A successful bid to join with Mexican state oil company \uc2 Petroleos Mexicanos\uc2  in the Trion oil field in the Gulf of Mexico should provide "a much-needed boost" to the miner's oil output, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . It tips BHP's volumes to fall from around 214 million BOE in FY17 to 186 million in FY20, and "Trion could lift BHP's oil volumes to back above 200 million BOE," the broker says. BHP trades up 1.2% at A$26.28/share. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle)\par \par \uc2 2304 GMT -- \uc2 Iluka Resources Ltd\uc2 . (ILU.AU) will have one key focus following the takeover of \uc2 Sierra Rutile\uc2 : getting mining costs down in its new business. Iluka plans "to introduce high energy scrubbing circuits, expand use of slurry and field pumps, and enable more direct dumping of ore," steps that "should help minimize stockpile reclaim and road haulage," says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . Iluka will also target increased recoveries from its mineral separation plant, the broker notes, following an investor call. Iluka last traded at A$6.95/share. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; Twitter: @RhiannonHoyle)\par \par \uc2 2246 GMT -- The risk to Australia's banks from regulatory pressures has diminished in recent months, Macquarie reckons. Changes to Net Stable Funding Calculations have improved the banks' ratios and reduced the risk of aggressive deposit competition, it says. Further, recent rhetoric suggests capital requirements are likely to be less onerous than previously anticipated, reducing the possible need for significant increases in banks' capital. Macquarie says it also believes an expected announcement from the Basel committee in January is likely to reinforce its view. As such, the brokerage continues to see scope for bank shares to outperform so it maintains an overweight stance on the sector, with \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  (NAB.AU) and \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) its preferred exposures. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 2245 GMT -- Private-equity firm \uc2 Bain Capital\uc2  has struck a deal to buy Camp Australia, a company providing before and after-school care in schools across Australia. The move bolsters Bain's education portfolio and comes on the heels of its acquisition of a majority stake in early-childhood company Only About Children. It also comes after Bain recently opened a Sydney office, a recognition of the importance of the market to its global portfolio. Managing Director Ed Han said there is an opportunity to grow Camp Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  further, and Bain planned to invest in the company's management, staff and services. The financial terms of the private deal with Camp Australia weren't disclosed. It is expected to close in early 2017. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 11 Dec 2016 23:55 ET  Global Equities Roundup: Market Talk -2-\par \par \uc2 2243 GMT -- \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  has been picked by the government of Australia's eastern New South Wales state to handle the combined investment funds of three agencies. The bank has been selected as preferred custodian for the investment assets of NSW Treasury Corp., SAS Trustees Corp. and Insurance & Care NSW. NSW Treasury has A$70 billion in assets under management. \uc2 Gladys Berejiklian\uc2 , the state treasurer, said the deal with \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  is expected to drive A$80 million in cost savings over 10 years, and also deliver more unified investment positions and best-practice financial management. \uc2 KPMG\uc2  was contracted by the treasury at a cost of A$1.4 million to assist with due diligence and other processes involved in selecting a custodian. The agreement is subject to final contract negotiations. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 2204 GMT -- \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  lifts building materials supplier \uc2 Boral\uc2  (BLD.AU) to equal-weight from underweight, mainly because the share price now more accurately reflects risks tied to its US$1.86-billion acquisition of \uc2 Headwaters Inc\uc2 . (HW). \uc2 Boral\uc2  raised around A$1.58 billion through an institutional placement and entitlement offer to fund the deal. "Although headline P/E is low, we continue to see some data points in the near term that stop us from moving overweight," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says. U.S. macro data are likely to remain challenging, the broker says, while earnings risks in Australia may be crystallized in the 1H result in February. "We also see some risks around regulatory approval for the \uc2 Headwaters\uc2  acquisition, which creates further near-term uncertainty," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says. BLD last traded at A$5.34, just below \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's A$5.58/share price target. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2159 GMT -- After cancer drug developer Sirtex's (SRX.AU) profit warning, Macquarie says the most concerning element of the downgrade is the U.S. weakness given that's historically been the growth engine for the company. While Sirtex called out increased competition in liver-directed therapies, Macquarie attributes the majority of the weakness to the imminent release of overall survival data from studies of its SIR-Spheres product, which the broker says may be leading to patient deferrals. "With (studies) FOXFIRE, SARAH and SIRveNIB all set to report overall survival data in 1H CY17, clinicians are likely hesitant to refer a patient for SIR-Spheres given the short-term potential for a negative read-out." Positive data from the study readouts early next year could be a catalyst for growth to return to previous levels, the broker says. (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2117 GMT -- \uc2 UBS\uc2  says the share price reaction to cancer drug developer Sirtex Medical's (SRX.AU) profit warning was overdone. Sirtex shares slumped 37% to A$16.00 on Friday after management forecast a 9-16% fall in 1H profit due to issues including increased competition for patients with liver-directed therapies. "Sirtex has a track record of volatility around alternate yet inferior new therapy, but the longer survival trumps," says \uc2 UBS\uc2 , reiterating its buy call on the stock. The drug company encountered similar issues in 2012, but returned to growth after the impact was annualized. \uc2 UBS\uc2  continues to expect data from two key drug trials in 2016. "Our discounted cash flow valuation approach remains indifferent to trial outcome and short-term volatility," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. Still, its price target moves down 14% to A$33.50/share. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The cost for banks in Hong Kong to borrow yuan from each other overnight rises to 5.467% on Monday, compared with 3.30586% on Friday. The overnight rate has been volatile in recent sessions, spiking above 12% on December 5. While the recent rise in overnight rates pales in comparison to the more-than 66% hit in early January, those rates remain higher and more choppy than rates in major financial markets. Higher short-term borrowing costs are viewed as a way to deter negative bets on the yuan, since it makes it more expensive for investors to short the yuan. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; Twitter: @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 0326 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Ziwo Holdings\uc2  (I9T.SG) shares surge 94.4% to S$0.035 after the synthetic yarn maker buys a 45% equity stake in a Singapore-based firm that produces charging equipment for electric vehicles. The company, Estar Investments, also has a unit in China, which can potentially help Ziwo grow its business rapidly. Ziwo says late Friday that the acquisition is in line with its diversification plans. With pollution concerns pushing car makers and governments to invest in electric vehicles, investors likely believe that Ziwo could ride the growth of Estar. Ziwo's rise contrasts with a 0.5% fall in the local benchmark FTSE Straits Times Index. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0318 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Hang Seng Index reversed early gains on Monday, now trading down 1.1%. While energy stocks \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  and Cnooc are being buoyed by the higher oil prices, most other stocks are down. "There's a lot more wariness about the FOMC meeting this week," said Andrew Sullivan, managing director of sales trading at \uc2 Haitong International Securities\uc2 . The popular opinion is that a U.S. Fed rate hike is 100% built in, but the concern now is how much the increase will be, he added. "Are they behind...the curve or are they going to kill the recovery?" Sullivan said about the dilemma the U.S. Fed faces. Shoe maker Belle International is among the biggest blue-chip decliners, with its shares down 6%, while \uc2 China Shenhua Energy\uc2  falls 3%. (willa.plank@wsj.com; Twitter: @willaplank)\par \par \uc2 0304 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The yuan exchange rate against the U.S. dollar could drop by about 5% next year, extending this year's weakness, says Zhao Yang, an economist at Nomura. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will unlikely allow a sharp depreciation in yuan rate. Instead, it will keep letting it depreciate from time to time and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it weakens too rapidly," he says. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is facing chronic capital outflows, not a short-term flight of hot money, as companies and households increase their overseas investments. The yuan has softened by more than 6% this year against the greenback. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0302 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China Green's (0904.HK) shares are under pressure, after it flagged a wider fiscal net loss for the first half. Shares were last down 8.5% at HK$0.193, underperforming the Hang Seng Index's 0.8% drop. The Hong Kong-listed seller of agriculture products said Friday it expects the net loss for the six months ended Oct. 31 to widen "substantially" from the CNY33.96 million a year earlier, as revenue from fresh produce and processed foods businesses fell amid a rise in operating expenses. The company said it expects to announce its results on Dec. 16. (chester.yung@wsj.com; Twitter: @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0259 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shares in \uc2 mm2 Asia\uc2  (1B0.SG) gain 1.2% to S$0.425 as the movies and media content producer tries to drum up some excitement in the guise of celebrating two years of being listed in Singapore. The company is offering free movie tickets and a voucher worth S$38 to its shareholders. Shareholders after a cut-off date will be entitled to a pair of tickets each for two new releases from the company and also get a chance to meet the film stars at a special screening. The two releases are likely to help boost the company's shares that have been on a decline in the past one month. mm2's gain contrasts with a 0.3% fall in Singapore's benchmark Straits Times Index. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 11, 2016 23:55 ET (04:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161212eccc000e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
