\par\par \b \uc2 Insurer Crackdown Sends China Shares Tumbling -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2016\par 05:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0427 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shanghai stocks tumbled the most in six months after regulators banned an insurer from buying stocks. The Shanghai Composite Index slumped 2% Monday, as nearly 1,000 stocks retreat. Last Friday, the insurance regulator banned the insurance unit of China's largest property developer, China Evergrande (3333.HK), from buying stocks, citing rules violations. Later Friday, Foresea Life, the arm of Chinese financial conglomerate \uc2 Baoneng Group\uc2  pledged to cut its holdings in Gree, a Shenzhen-listed home-appliance heavyweight stock. This would see it unwind stake-building that drew harsh disapproval from Chinese regulators. "It's clear that the regulator is strongly against such stake buying by insurers that may disturb management of listed firms," said Deng Wenyuan, an analyst at Soochow Securities, "Investors did not expect a sudden tightening up over insurers." (yifan.xie@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Chinese equity markets tumble Monday, with the main boards in Shanghai and Shenzhen down 2% and 2.85% respectively. The tech start-up ChiNext board in Shenzhen is down 3.11%. There are two pieces of news hitting sentiment: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's suggestions over the weekend that there may be a change in the U.S.'s recognition of the One-China policy, a core policy that has maintained cross-Straits peace for decades, as well as rising inflation pressure, says Hao Hong, managing director of research at Bank of Communications. "People were expecting trade disputes but not fundamental differences in ideologies between the two nations," said Hong, in reference to Trump's surprising comments questioning the One China Policy. The rise in steel, iron ore and coking coal prices are all signalling that inflation is on the rise, which will put pressure on both stocks and bonds. The yield on China's 10-year bond rose to 3.122% from 3.097% Friday. Yields rise as prices fall.(anjie.zheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @anjiezheng)\par \par \uc2 0354 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Lowering the annual economic growth target is not an option for Beijing in 2017, as stability will likely be the key policy goal ahead of the 19th Communist Party congress, says \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2 . The authorities will likely to keep the growth target unchanged at a range of between 6.5% and 7.0%, the bank says, and expects the country's gross domestic product to grow by 6.6% next year. The dollar/yuan pair will likely top 7.0 by mid-2017, it says in a research note. China will move toward a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime over the next two to three years, the house says. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0340 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] A rebound in China's Producer Price Index for November from a year earlier implies strong revenue growth for metals and mining companies, says Argonaut Securities. China's producer-price index rose 3.3% in November from a year earlier, compared with a 1.2% on-year increase in October. The PPI increased 1.5% in November from a month earlier. Coal, steel and other commodity prices have rebounded amid increased Chinese demand fueled by infrastructure spending. Copper and aluminum smelters and steel producers in China will see their profits growing because of higher revenues, Argonaut adds. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0338 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shares of Japanese bank shares are among decliners Monday morning after the sector rose for four consecutive days last week. The rise followed a solid gain in financial stocks in the U.S. amid bullish sentiment in the financial sector after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's victory. The banking subsector in Tokyo market has rose about 35% since November 9. Analysts say Japanese banks have underperformed the market for a long time and will likely continue to pick up, while some bank executives say the current rally may not be sustainable if uncertainty over Europe triggers another mess beyond the continent. MUFG (8306.TO) is down 3.3% at Y737.4, \uc2 Mizuho FG (8411.TO) \uc2 is off 1.8% at Y217.8 while SMFG (8316.TO) sinks 2.4% to Y4,615. (atsuko.fukase@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  expects the U.S. Fed to deliver a 25 basis point rate hike on December 14 and another rate hike in 2017. The rate rises could quicken in 2018 with as many as four rate hikes as more hawkish Board members likely land on the FOMC, and as Chairman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is seen as likely stepping down in early 2018, the bank adds. The bank predicts he Fed Board will conduct business as usual after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's U.S. presidential victory. "We think the FOMC will want to avoid introducing unnecessary volatility after the sharp market gyrations in recent weeks, and avoid unnecessary meddling in politics," the bank says, adding the status quo could be a 'relatively safe option'. Furthermore, the Fed's balance sheet could be allowed to shrink 'passively' from early 2018 after being stuck at US$4.5 trillion since late 2014, the bank adds. (chester.yung@wsj.com; Twitter: @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The cost for banks in Hong Kong to borrow yuan from each other overnight rises to 5.467% on Monday, compared with 3.30586% on Friday. The overnight rate has been volatile in recent sessions, spiking above 12% on December 5. While the recent rise in overnight rates pales in comparison to the more-than 66% hit in early January, those rates remain higher and more choppy than rates in major financial markets. Higher short-term borrowing costs are viewed as a way to deter negative bets on the yuan, since it makes it more expensive for investors to short the yuan. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; Twitter: @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] South Korea's household-credit boom is unlikely to go bust anytime soon, with most of the debt taken on by the more wealthy households. Korea's statistical office says in a report Monday families in the highest income bracket owe financial institutions more than a dozen times as much debt as those in the lowest bracket. It says 71.3% of the richest group borrows bank loans, sitting on an average of 98.5 million won ($84,2000) per household. Of families in the lowest-income bracket, only 25.8% use bank loans with the average household debt standing at 7.9 million won. The household credit rose 3% on quarter to a record 1.296 quadrillion won in the third quarter of 2016, faster than a revised 2.8% gain in the second quarter. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0324 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The yield on the 40-year Japanese government bond is rising sharply again Monday, continuing a trend seen in recent days. The 40-year JGB is yielding 0.885%, up from 0.82% Friday. If the movement continues in this direction for another few days, the yield could hit 1% for the first time since Feb. 24. The moves are giving a sharper slope to Japan's yield curve since shorter-term rates continue to be negative or close to zero, and that in turn is good news for banks and insurance companies that profit for a steeper yield curve. (peter.landers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0304 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The yuan exchange rate against the U.S. dollar could drop by about 5% next year, extending this year's weakness, says Zhao Yang, an economist at Nomura. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will unlikely allow a sharp depreciation in yuan rate. Instead, it will keep letting it depreciate from time to time and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it weakens too rapidly," he says. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is facing chronic capital outflows, not a short-term flight of hot money, as companies and households increase their overseas investments. The yuan has softened by more than 6% this year against the greenback. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0258 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] All signs seem to be blinking "reflation" these days, with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's expansive spending plans and higher oil prices, which shot up Monday after a weekend deal to cut production. But the trend may not prove lasting, writes Frederic Neumann, co-head of Asian economics research at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Hong Kong. "This doesn't look like a sustained return of price pressures, at least in Asia," he says. While various inflation readings are likely to surprise to the upside, gauges will normalize by mid-2017 and level out. Here's why: First, given the crash in oil prices early last year, the base effects of rising prices will be stark initially, but then start to stabilize in April. Second, a weaker Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will export deflation, not inflation, as cheaper Chinese goods force competitors to slash prices. Third, weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will only spur inflation if growth responds in stride, and that looks doubtful. Therefire, don't read any uptick in inflation gauges "as a sign of a sustained acceleration in demand, without which price pressures cannot climb for a prolonged period. Central bankers will look through all this, and so should you," says Neumann. (rachel.rosenthal@wsj.com; Twitter: @rosenthalrc)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Metal prices extended gains in Asia trade as positive signs of China's economy overode a stronger dollar. China's consumer inflation picked up for a third straight month in November, while industrial inflation came in much stronger than expected. Hopes of greater U.S. investment in infrastructure following \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's election win have also fed into the optimism on metals, though some analysts believe the bullish sentiment is now overdone. Three-month copper and aluminum prices on the \uc2 London Metal Exchange\uc2  both rose 0.6% to $5,862.5/ton and $1,760/ton respectively, while zinc and nickel were also up by 0.9% and 0.8% at $2,734.5/ton and $11,565/ton. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 11, 2016 23:27 ET (04:27 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161212eccc0008l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
