\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2016\par 04:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The cost for banks in Hong Kong to borrow yuan from each other overnight rises to 5.467% on Monday, compared with 3.30586% on Friday. The overnight rate has been volatile in recent sessions, spiking above 12% on December 5. While the recent rise in overnight rates pales in comparison to the more-than 66% hit in early January, those rates remain higher and more choppy than rates in major financial markets. Higher short-term borrowing costs are viewed as a way to deter negative bets on the yuan, since it makes it more expensive for investors to short the yuan. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; Twitter: @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 0304 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The yuan exchange rate against the U.S. dollar could drop by about 5% next year, extending this year's weakness, says Zhao Yang, an economist at Nomura. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will unlikely allow a sharp depreciation in yuan rate. Instead, it will keep letting it depreciate from time to time and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it weakens too rapidly," he says. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is facing chronic capital outflows, not a short-term flight of hot money, as companies and households increase their overseas investments. The yuan has softened by more than 6% this year against the greenback. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Metal prices extended gains in Asia trade as positive signs of China's economy overode a stronger dollar. China's consumer inflation picked up for a third straight month in November, while industrial inflation came in much stronger than expected. Hopes of greater U.S. investment in infrastructure following \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's election win have also fed into the optimism on metals, though some analysts believe the bullish sentiment is now overdone. Three-month copper and aluminum prices on the \uc2 London Metal Exchange\uc2  both rose 0.6% to $5,862.5/ton and $1,760/ton respectively, while zinc and nickel were also up by 0.9% and 0.8% at $2,734.5/ton and $11,565/ton. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shipments of flexible displays will more than double next year compared to this year as smartphone makers increasingly turn to curved-screen handsets to lure new customers, says Jerry Kang, a display research analyst for IHS Markit. Much of the jump, to a forecasted 139 million units next year, can be attributed to Apple (APPL), which is expected to begin using flexible displays for its iPhone next year. From there, \uc2 IHS\uc2  expects shipments to roughly double again in 2018, eventually leveling out at about 560 million shipments in 2023, versus 59 million shipments this year. On the supply side, \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 ' (005930.SE) display unit and \uc2 LG Display\uc2  (034220.SE) are quickly ramping up production of flexible displays, which could head off any concerns of a bottleneck. (jonathan.cheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @jchengwsj)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Singapore dollar is lower against its U.S. counterpart early Monday buyoed by sentiment that the U.S. Fed will raise interest rate at the meeting of its rate-setting panel on Tuesday and Wednesday. "The U.S. dollar remains strong," Margaret Yang, market analyst at CMC Markets says. Higher U.S. interest rates pressure emerging-market nations by making their assets less attractive to yield-seeking investors and by making their debt more expensive to pay back. "The U.S. dollar is looking for the Fed to signal more hikes in 2017 at its the Federal Open Market Committee meeting," DBS says. "The greenback's extension will depend on the Fed's guidance for its 2017 rate trajectory." The U.S. dollar is at S$1.4320. The pair closed Friday at S$1.4301. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; Twitter: @journosaurabh)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] NZD/USD Intraday: Downside prevails. The pair accelerated on the downside after it broke below its 20-period and 50-period moving averages. The relative strength index is below its neutrality level at 50 and lacks upward momentum. Therefore, as long as 0.7160 holds on the upside, look for a further drop toward 0.7100. A break below this level would call for a new decline toward 0.7070. Alternatively, above 0.7160, look for further upsides with 0.7190 and 0.7210 as targets. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0116 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] AUD/USD Intraday: Under pressure. The pair remains on the downside, backed by its descending 20-period and 50-period moving averages. The relative strength index is bearish below its neutrality area at 50. In addition, the process of lower highs and lows remains intact, which should confirm a negative outlook. Hence, as long as 0.7460 holds as a resistance, likely decline to 0.7425 and 0.7410 in extension. Alternatively, above 0.7460, expect further upsides with 0.7475 and 0.7490 as targets. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0114 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] EUR/GBP Intraday: Key resistance at 0.8415. The pair stays below its key resistance at 0.8415 and remains on the downside. The declining 50-period moving average maintains a bearish bias, and the relative strength index is bearish and calls for further decline. As long as 0.8415 holds as the key resistance, the risk of a drop toward 0.8345 remains high. Alternatively, a break above 0.8415 would call for a bounce toward 0.8460 at first. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0111 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/CAD Intraday: Downside prevails. The pair has been capped by a negative trend line, and is currently accelerating on the downside. Meanwhile the relative strength index has broken below its 30 level. The immediate trend is down and the momentum is strong. As long as 1.3185 is not broken above, a break below 1.3100 is likely. Alternatively, a stand above 1.3185 would allow for a challenge of 1.3235. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9] (analysts@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 1909 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] South Korea's government said Sunday that it would enact measures to reassure foreign investors that last Friday's impeachment legislative motion to bring down President Park Geun-hye will not affect the country's financial markets. At a press briefing, Yoo Il-ho, the country's deputy prime minister for economic affairs, said that Seoul's policy stance will be "maintained in a consistent manner" and that the impeachment's impact on the market will be "largely negligible," adding that market indicators such as the won-dollar exchange rate has remained stable. South Korea's budget for next year, passed earlier this month, will also be implemented as planned and the government will announce its upcoming economic policies before the end of the year, said Mr. Yoo. (eun-young.jeong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 11, 2016 22:29 ET (03:29 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161212eccc0002b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
