\par\par \b \uc2 China's Yuan and the Trillion-Dollar Numbers Game -- Heard on the Street\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nathaniel Taplin \par \uc2 342 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2016\par 11:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Another month, another $70 billion in reserves gone. Life at China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator has gotten less comfortable again.\par \par \uc2 China's official measure of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell $69 billion in November, the biggest drop since January, when the plunging yuan rocked global markets. The overall level is now perilously close to the $3 trillion mark, the lowest since 2011.\par \par \uc2 The fall last month had partially to do with measurement effects. Although China doesn't publish the official composition of its reserves, analysts estimate that about 60% is in dollars, with the remainder in other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the euro and the yen. When the dollar rises against those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it did in November, the overall measure of reserves, given in dollars, falls.\par \par \uc2 Yet valuation effects alone should account for a drop in reserves of around $20 billion in November, according to Julian Evans-Pritchard at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . That means the drop due to net-reserve sales was around $50 billion. Beijing let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  take some of the pain, with the yuan's relatively steep 1.6% decline against the dollar last month. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  forcefully to stem the damage in recent weeks. While \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has drawn President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's ire for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, it is arguably doing Mr. Trump and the U.S. economy a favor by preventing the yuan from falling even further.\par \par \uc2 Outflows undoubtedly remain strong, judging from Beijing's recent crackdown on capital leaving the country. An article carried Tuesday on China's official newswire said officials were closely monitoring "irrational" overseas investment.\par \par \uc2 There may be nothing significant per se about having more than $3 trillion in reserves. But markets have a way of getting antsy about these things. Another $50 billion drop and China will once again see what life is like in the $2 trillion club.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 07, 2016 05:27 ET (10:27 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161207ecc70019s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
