\par\par \b \uc2 Market bears smell blood as China scrambles to plug capital outflows\b0\par\par\uc2 1,122 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2016\par 00:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Concerns grow falling yuan will encourage capital outflows\par \par \uc2 * Yuan dropped to lowest in more than eight years against dollar\par \par \uc2 * Has edged up since on suspected state-led \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves aggressively to plug capital outflows\par \par \uc2 * Fund managers see continued slide in yuan against dollar\par \par \uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan and Jennifer Ablan\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE/NEW YORK, Dec 5 (Reuters) - China's increasingly aggressive measures to clampdown on capital outflows will not have gone unnoticed by U.S. hedge-fund manager Kyle Bass.\par \par \uc2 Bass is famed for his successful bet against the U.S. housing market in the global financial crisis, so markets monitor his comments closely. He has long argued the yuan, or renminbi (RMB), is set to fall 30 percent against the dollar and he sees capital outflows as backing his view.\par \par \uc2 "China's capital outflows are worse than they appear, which is why the government has allowed the RMB to depreciate over the last two months," Bass told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "We believe this pressure will continue with the prospect for higher interest rates in the U.S."\par \par \uc2 China is trying to tighten its grip on capital outflows after a slide in the yuan this year of almost 6 percent, which has pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to levels last seen more than eight years ago and revived memories of a wave of capital flight late last year and in January.\par \par \uc2 China's Vice Finance Minister Zhu Guangyao was quoted on Saturday as saying policymakers were watching capital outflows closely.\par \par \uc2 Bank of China, the country's biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading bank, has begun to sharply limit corporate customers' ability to purchase \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Shanghai, sources said on Friday. Customers who insist on buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are being restricted to $1 million, compared with no caps previously.\par \par \uc2 Among other moves, the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) is vetting transfers abroad of $5 million or more, down from $50 million previously, and is stepping up scrutiny of major outbound deals, sources said.\par \par \uc2 The yuan slumped to more than 6.92 per dollar in late November before suspected state-directed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a lift.\par \par \uc2 It was trading just under 6.90 on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The slide has sparked a flurry of bets against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A Reuters poll shows those bets increased to their most since January - the tail end of China's 2015 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street bank \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says buying the dollar against the yuan is its number two recommended trade for 2017, after bets against sterling and the euro. Since the yuan is closely controlled onshore, it suggests buying the dollar against the yuan in 12-month forward contracts offshore.\par \par \uc2 The one-year non-deliverable forward contract currently prices the yuan at 7.1 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong-based Luke Spajic, head of portfolio management for emerging Asia at bond fund PIMCO, sees a steady fall in the yuan.\par \par \uc2 "It's reasonable to expect a depreciation of between 5 to 7 percent per year as a rough guide," Spajic said.\par \par \uc2 Most speculative trade in the yuan takes place offshore given China's capital controls. Aside from derivatives markets and ETFs, speculators invest in assets of economies with heavy trade exposure to the country, like the Australian or Taiwan dollars and Hong Kong listings of Chinese mainland companies.\par \par \uc2 RISKY CYCLE\par \par \uc2 Illicit capital outflows have increased as a concern for the government this year as it attempted to put the economy back on track and keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable without unduly draining its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of more than $3 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Chinese regulations grant individuals an annual \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  quota of $50,000 a year, a factor on the radar of markets as 2017 looms. A survey by the Hurun report, a monthly magazine best known for its "China's Rich List", showed 60 percent of wealthy Chinese planned to buy property overseas in the next three years.\par \par \uc2 Many individuals move larger sums offshore by tapping relatives' quotas and companies also sneak huge amounts of cash offshore through fake invoicing.\par \par \uc2 In October, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves slumped more than $45 billion - the most since January or the tail end of China's stock market crisis. November data is due on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Although the fall in reserves in October was mainly seen as reflective of the yuan's declining value, a researcher at \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned the slide and capital flight could feed off of one another.\par \par \uc2 "Depreciation triggers capital flight, and capital flight exerts even bigger pressure on the yuan," Wang Zhenying, head of the Statistics and Research Department of the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 's (\uc2 PBOC\uc2 ) Shanghai Head Office, said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "Therefore, it's necessary to break this feedback loop," he said.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say there are fundamental reasons suggesting the yuan should fall. While economic growth has steadied this year at around 6.7 percent, it is seen sliding next year. The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  forecasts growth of 6.2 percent in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Still, the yuan is falling alongside other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the face of a dollar that is surging on expectations of higher interest rates as Trump tries to lift the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 But China's relationship with the United States is highly uncertain. Trump has promised to name China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator on his first day in office in January and to slap 45 percent import tariffs on Chinese goods.\par \par \uc2 He prompted protests from China this weekend by speaking by phone on Friday with Taiwan's president, something no previous U.S. president had done since the United States switched its diplomatic recognition to China from Taiwan in 1979.\par \par \uc2 SCEPTICAL CHINESE\par \par \uc2 Investors said the pressure on the yuan is nowhere near as extensive as it was late last year when a stock market slump and surprise devaluation of the yuan raised fears that the economy was in worse health than Beijing had let on.\par \par \uc2 Concerns about China's debt mountain have also eased this year and the government has underpinned economic growth with a massive stimulus effort.\par \par \uc2 It has also kept the yuan largely stable in trade-weighted terms, although analysts said that may be lost on many Chinese who tend to focus more on the dollar rate.\par \par \uc2 "When you know your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to be weaker, there is going to be a desire to try to take advantage of that or to try to hedge, so the desire to get dollars will still be there. It is natural to see these kinds of capital outflows," PIMCO's Spajic said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Vidya Ranganathan in SINGAPORE and Jennifer Ablan in NEW YORK: Editing by Neil Fullick) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-12-5T00:00:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161204ecc400op3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
