\par\par \b \uc2 China Struggles to Steady Yuan's Decline\b0\par\par\uc2 848 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2016\par 13:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 China is facing an uphill battle to maintain an orderly depreciation of the yuan as investors pile up bearish bets against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside the mainland.\par \par \uc2 The gap between the yuan's value against the dollar in the domestic market and in what is known as the offshore market in Hong Kong, has been widening in recent days. On Wednesday, this so-called spread reached 0.0333, its widest since the beginning of October (apart from the day after the U.S. election), although it narrowed a touch on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 While the Chinese authorities strictly limit the way the yuan trades at home, it can be bought and sold more freely in Hong Kong. But its value against the dollar is usually about the same in both markets.\par \par \uc2 The widening gap now is complicating the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strategy of letting some air out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a pace Beijing dictates. The two yuan markets at home and in Hong Kong often feed off each other. Moreover, a weaker yuan offshore could encourage more Chinese businesses and individuals\u8212\'20\'14the mainstay of the mainland market\u8212\'20\'14to seek to convert their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into dollars, potentially adding downward pressure on the domestically traded yuan.\par \par \uc2 China has tolerated a weaker yuan since early October, right after its entry into the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's elite group of reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acknowledging that a cheaper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the price of using easy money to prop up the economy.\par \par \uc2 The pace of depreciation has quickened since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's surprise U.S. presidential-election win sent the greenback soaring and emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbling. The yuan is down 6.2% against the dollar this year in onshore markets, reaching 6.9152 against the dollar on Thursday, with more than a third of the drop in the past two weeks.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  is unwilling to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slide too far, too fast, for fear that might lead to destabilizing capital flows out of the country.\par \par \uc2 Investors in offshore markets have, though, been pounding the yuan weaker, in the apparent belief that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may struggle to control the pace of its decline.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 " to shore up the yuan, said Prashant Singh, a senior portfolio manager at \uc2 Neuberger Berman\uc2  in Singapore. But he said he expects the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall further because of the gloomy outlook for global trade and the diverging economic trajectories in the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -led \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are typically temporary in nature," Mr. Singh added. "The fundamental factors still point to weakness."\par \par \uc2 Already, in the past week, some state-owned banks\u8212\'20\'14which often act as proxy for the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14have sold dollars to support the yuan after it dropped to its lowest level against the dollar in eight years onshore, traders say.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been setting the yuan's daily official rate against the dollar, known as the "fix," somewhat stronger than implied by the greenback's strength against other global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days, according to analysts, indicating its desire to avoid too sharp of a decline. The yuan hasn't fallen as much against the dollar as have other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the yen, the euro and the Korean won recently.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, when the gap between the yuan's value at home and in Hong Kong also widened, the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  directed state-owned banks to buy large quantities of yuan in the Hong Kong market, sending overnight borrowing costs to elevated levels that effectively prohibited investors from wagering against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This time around, however, short-term overnight borrowing costs in Hong Kong have remained around levels that analysts consider normal, suggesting little \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  offshore.\par \par \uc2 One factor that might have limited \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively, analysts say, is its shrinking \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The stockpile, still the world's largest of its kind, fell to $3.12 trillion last month, its lowest since 2011. That compares to a record $4 trillion in June 2014.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts within China, including prominent economist Yu Yongding, are now urging the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to adopt a more flexible exchange-rate regime to help the yuan find its true market level.\par \par \uc2 The costs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they say, have become too high and could encourage capital flight. They have called on Beijing to take the step before Mr. Trump takes office in January: The New York businessman has pledged to label China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator and to slap higher tariffs on Chinese goods.\par \par \uc2 "We have capital controls as the last line of defense," Mr. Yu wrote in an article published in state media this week. "We don't need to worry too much about the short-term depreciation of the yuan."\par \par \uc2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com and Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 24, 2016 07:25 ET (12:25 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161124ecbo001cb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
