\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Markets Closed for Holiday\b0\par\par\uc2 2,428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2016\par 23:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MARKETS AT A GLANCE \par (Data as of approximately 10 a.m. ET Thursday) \par  \par                     LAST        CHANGE  % CHG \par DJIA                19083.18    59.31   0.31% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2               5380.68     -5.67   -0.11% \par S&P 500             2204.72     1.78    0.08% \par Japan: Nikkei 225   18333.41    170.47  0.94% \par Hang Seng           22608.49    -68.20  -0.30% \par Shanghai Composite  3241.74     0.60    0.02% \par S&P \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sensex      25860.17    -191.64 -0.74% \par Australia: S&P/ASX  5485.10     0.70    0.01% \par UK: FTSE 100        6814.91     -2.80   -0.04% \par  \par                     PRICE CHG   YIELD% \par U.S. 2 Year         0/32        1.126 \par U.S. 5 Year         0/32        1.835 \par U.S. 10 Year        0/32        2.353 \par Australia 10 Year   -16/32      2.768 \par China 10 Year       4/32        2.866 \par India 10 Year       9/32        6.270 \par Japan 10 Year       -2/32       0.036 \par German 10 Year      3/32        0.253 \par  \par                         LAST(MID)   CHANGE \par Australia $ (AUD/USD)   0.7407      0.0021 \par Yen (USD/JPY)           113.15      0.65 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW) 1179.72     -2.17 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)  6.9165      -0.0037 \par Euro (EUR/USD)          1.0572      0.0019 \par WSJ Dollar Index        91.90       0.03 \par  \par                 LAST    CHANGE  % CHG \par Crude Oil       48.05   0.09    0.19% \par Brent Crude     49.09   0.14    0.29% \par Gold            1189.4  -3.0    -0.25% \par  \par SNAPSHOT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. markets were closed Thursday for the Thanksgiving holiday. European stock markets were calm, while those in Asia were mixed. Oil was slightly higher in London ahead of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\f28 \uc2 \f2  meeting next week. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OPENING CALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest import data out of China and India show a mixed picture for gold demand in the two largest consumers, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Economics\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. China's appetite for gold appears to have faltered in recent months. Still, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Economics\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects China's demand to strengthen in coming months ahead of New Year holiday. Lower prices should also act as a further incentive for buyers. Meanwhile, "the measures introduced by the Indian government in November to tackle illegal activity seem to have prompted a surge in gold buying and we expect that they will continue to boost imports over the next few months." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EQUITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets calmed Thursday after Wall Street climbed to record highs for a third consecutive session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Stoxx Europe 600 edged up, following a mixed session in Asia. U.S. markets were closed Thursday for Thanksgiving, while the WSJ Dollar Index was little moved.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average, the S&P 500 and the Russell 2000 all closed at record highs in light trading on Wednesday, while the dollar climbed to its highest since 2002 on expectations for stronger U.S. growth and higher interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The reflationary theme is sucking money back into the U.S. stock market and injecting huge shock waves through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets," said Jane Foley, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , referring to expectations that tax cuts and less regulation will boost corporate profits and U.S. growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Thursday, markets in Asia mostly moved lower amid concerns that higher U.S. interest rates and a stronger dollar could hurt emerging markets and worsen capital flight from Asia. South Korea's KOSPI index fell 0.8%, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index fell 0.3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average advanced 0.9%, however, as a weaker yen boosted shares of exporters. The dollar gained 0.7% against the yen at 113.3390. The yen has fallen over 7% against the dollar so far this month. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 China is facing an uphill battle to maintain an orderly depreciation of the yuan as investors pile up bearish bets against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outside the mainland.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The gap between the yuan's value against the dollar in the domestic market and in what is known as the offshore market in Hong Kong, has been widening in recent days. On Wednesday, this so-called spread reached 0.0333, its widest since the beginning of October (apart from the day after the U.S. election), although it narrowed a touch on Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the Chinese authorities strictly limit the way the yuan trades at home, it can be bought and sold more freely in Hong Kong. But its value against the dollar is usually about the same in both markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The widening gap now is complicating the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strategy of letting some air out of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a pace Beijing dictates. The two yuan markets at home and in Hong Kong often feed off each other. Moreover, a weaker yuan offshore could encourage more Chinese businesses and individuals-the mainstay of the mainland market-to seek to convert their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into dollars, potentially adding downward pressure on the domestically traded yuan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has tolerated a weaker yuan since early October, right after its entry into the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 's elite group of reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , acknowledging that a cheaper \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is the price of using easy money to prop up the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pace of depreciation has quickened since \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2 's surprise U.S. presidential-election win sent the greenback soaring and emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tumbling. The yuan is down 6.2% against the dollar this year in onshore markets, closing lower Thursday. The dollar closed at 6.9189 yuan, compared with 6.8928 Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  is unwilling to let the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slide too far, too fast, for fear that might lead to destabilizing capital flows out of the country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors in offshore markets have, though, been pounding the yuan weaker, in the apparent belief that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may struggle to control the pace of its decline.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " to shore up the yuan, said Prashant Singh, a senior portfolio manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Neuberger Berman\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore. But he said he expects the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fall further because of the gloomy outlook for global trade and the diverging economic trajectories in the U.S. and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -led \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are typically temporary in nature," Mr. Singh added. "The fundamental factors still point to weakness."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro inched up against the dollar on Thursday after closing at its lowest against the dollar since March 2015, pressured both by the appreciation of the dollar and concerns over political risks in the eurozone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area's economy could be affected by U.S. trade policy changes and possible spillover effects from higher inflation and U.S. interest rate expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economic policies introduced by President-elect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  "will likely become more inward-oriented," the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  wrote in its biannual Financial Stability Review.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, the euro inched up against the dollar on Thursday after closing at its lowest against the dollar since March 2015, pressured both by the appreciation of the dollar and concerns over political risks in the eurozone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area's economy could be affected by U.S. trade policy changes and possible spillover effects from higher inflation and U.S. interest rate expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economic policies introduced by President-elect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  "will likely become more inward-oriented," the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  wrote in its biannual Financial Stability Review. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian credits were flat Thursday with very thin trade flow ahead of the Thanksgiving Day holiday in the U.S. Spreads of Asian investment-grade U.S.-dollar bonds over U.S. Treasurys were almost unchanged, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Aluminum International Engineering Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s newly issued perpetual bonds traded up, according to a bond trader.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "New issues remain in focus, especially Chinese issuers; those are rushing to the bond market before a likely Fed rate hike in December and Christmas holidays," the trader says. At least six issuers, mostly Chinese, have announced plans or are doing road shows this week for dollar bond sales.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, the yield on the 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 German government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond was steady, while 10-year Japanese government bond yields fell. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COMMODITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures were higher in London on Thursday ahead of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\f28 \uc2 \f2  meeting next week, where a deal to cut output is expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Headlines on \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  have been the dominant factor driving price moves in the oil market for much of this year. Prices have rebounded in recent weeks in anticipation that the cartel will follow through with a production cut at its Vienna meeting on Nov. 30, though some sticking points remain around the terms of a cut for members such as Iran and Iraq.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With a steady flow of supportive statements, member countries have raised the stakes and put themselves in a position where something needs be delivered," consultancy JBC Energy said in a research note, adding that a deal remains complex due to economic challenges in member states and geopolitical issues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold, meanwhile, was trading at its lowest level in 9 1/2 months on Thursday as the strong dollar and speculation about an Indian import ban continued to take the shine off the metal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For the short term, the precious complex will find it hard to recover with the dollar as strong as it is," David Govett, head of precious metals at brokerage firm Marex Spectron, wrote in a research note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Thursday, the WSJ Dollar Index, which weighs the dollar against a basket of other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was up slightly, building on a 14-year high on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper prices hit a 17-month high on Thursday morning in London and were up nearly 3% in late Asia trade as bullish expectations for manufacturing spending outweighed the dollar hitting multiyear highs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, the metal closed at a 16-month high in New York trading after U.S. durable goods data showed a 4.8% jump, the highest in a year, stoking further expectations of higher demand for the metal. American markets were closed on Thursday for the Thanksgiving holiday. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S HEADLINES \par  \par China's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Anbang Insurance\f28 \uc2 \f2  Plans First International Bond Issue \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 24 Nov 2016 17:29 ET \par Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Markets Closed for -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anbang is pursuing its first international bond offering as the acquisitive Chinese insurer raises money to fund its ambitions to grow abroad and at home, according to people familiar with the situation. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Ctrip, China's Biggest Travel Site, Goes Abroad for Its Latest Expansion \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the acquisition of British search company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Skyscanner\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Ctrip, whose expansion has been fueled by the Chinese middle class's ever-growing appetite for travel, will grow its own footprint overseas. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Indonesia Near Tax Deal for $73 Million or Less \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Alphabet's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  unit is nearing a settlement with Indonesian authorities over back taxes for no more than $73 million, a senior tax official said, far less than the up to $367 million Indonesia initially sought. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par South Korean Scandal Widens With New Raids, Impeachment Effort \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean prosecutors searched the offices of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lotte Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 SK Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  as part of their widening probe into an influence-peddling scandal involving President Park Geun-hye and her confidant Choi Soon-sil. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Goldman Seeks $2 Billion for South Korean Asset \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  is shopping one of South Korea's biggest industrial-gas producers to prospective buyers in a deal that could fetch $2 billion. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Turkey Blames Damascus for Strike That Killed Soldiers in Syria \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turkey's military said three of its soldiers were killed in Syria, blaming the Assad regime for a deadly airstrike that would mark Damascus's first assault on Turkish forces since Ankara launched its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Syria. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Saudi Arabia Backs OPEC Push to Make Steep Oil Output Cuts \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Saudi Arabia is backing an effort to make the steepest oil-production cuts possible at \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's meeting next week and to convince producers outside the cartel to help remove almost 2% of the world's oil supply, people familiar with the matter said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU Votes to Freeze Talks on Turkey Joining the Bloc \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Parliament\f28 \uc2 \f2  voted overwhelmingly to temporarily freeze talks on Turkey's bid to join the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , citing deteriorating human rights and democratic standards under President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's rule. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Scaffolding Collapses at China Power Plant, Killing 67 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The collapse of construction scaffolding at a power plant under expansion in eastern China killed at least 67 people, state media said, the latest deadly accident in a country grappling with poor industrial safety. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Retailers Push Early Start to Black Friday Sales \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the battle for shoppers, traditional retailers have been moving discounts earlier in November, linking their stores and websites more closely and finding ways to capitalize on the rise in mobile shopping. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par U.S., Western Allies Push Iran on New Measures to Bolster Nuclear Deal \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. and its Western allies are pressing Iran to take steps to sharply cut the amount of radioactive material it holds in a bid to shore up last year's nuclear deal and discourage the incoming Trump administration from abandoning it, Western officials said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Iraq Commanders Weigh Tactical Shift in Mosul \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Iraq's top military commanders are lobbying Prime Minister Haider al-Abadi to shift tactics, officials said, as the initial lightning advance toward Iraq's second biggest city turns into a perilous urban slog, with gains measured in feet rather than miles. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par (Times in GMT, followed by country and event) \par  \par 2100    SKA Nov Consumer Sentiment Index \par 2145    NZ Oct DELAYED: Overseas Merchandise Trade, date to be advised following recent earthquake in New Zealand \par 2330    JPN Oct CPI (Nation), CPI ex-food (Nation) \par 2330    JPN Nov CPI (Tokyo), CPI ex-Food (Tokyo) \par 2350    JPN Oct Services Producer Price Index \par 0001    UK Nov Hometrack UK Cities House Price Index