\par\par \b \uc2 Australian Morning Briefing: U.S. Markets Closed for Holiday\b0\par\par\uc2 3,627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2016\par 21:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par DJ Indus     19083.18  +59.31 +0.31% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2         5380.68   -5.67 -0.11% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Comp    10835.90  +15.72 +0.15% \par S&P 500       2204.72   +1.78 +0.08% \par Russell 2000  1342.09   +7.75 +0.58% \par TSM          22957.58  +34.23 +0.15% \par S&P/TSX      15080.91  -19.47 -0.13% \par FTSE 100      6804.34  -13.37 -0.20% \par FTSE 250     17558.26  -64.26 -0.36% \par Xetra DAX    10668.39   +5.95 +0.06% \par CAC40         4528.90   -0.31 -0.01% \par Nikkei Stock 18333.41 +170.47 +0.94% \par Hang Seng    22608.49  -68.20 -0.30% \par S&P/ASX 200   5485.10   +0.70 +0.01% \par Global Stock Indexes at 1325 GMT. \par  \par  \par INTERBANK \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RATES: \par  \par            Latest  Previous % Chg \par                       Close \par USD/JPY 113.17-18 112.50-51 +0.60 \par EUR/USD 1.0557-60 1.0551-54 +0.06 \par GBP/USD 1.2454-56 1.2441-43 +0.10 \par USD/CHF 1.0166-70 1.0165-69 +0.01 \par USD/CAD 1.3491-96 1.3486-91 +0.04 \par AUD/USD 0.7388-92 0.7384-88 +0.05 \par NZD/USD 0.6991-97 0.7002-08 -0.16 \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates at 1250 GMT. \par  \par TREASURYS: \par 2Y Tsy  99 24/32    -3/32 1.135%  +0.4BPS \par 5Y Tsy  99 19/32    -9/32 1.835%  +5.8BPS \par 10Y Tsy 96 28/32    -10/32 2.355%  +3.6BPS \par  \par U.S. STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. markets were closed Thursday for the Thanksgiving holiday. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China is facing an uphill battle to maintain an orderly depreciation of the yuan as investors pile up bearish bets against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outside the mainland.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The gap between the yuan's value against the dollar in the domestic market and in what is known as the offshore market in Hong Kong, has been widening in recent days. On Wednesday, this so-called spread reached 0.0333, its widest since the beginning of October (apart from the day after the U.S. election), although it narrowed a touch on Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the Chinese authorities strictly limit the way the yuan trades at home, it can be bought and sold more freely in Hong Kong. But its value against the dollar is usually about the same in both markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The widening gap now is complicating the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strategy of letting some air out of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a pace Beijing dictates. The two yuan markets at home and in Hong Kong often feed off each other. Moreover, a weaker yuan offshore could encourage more Chinese businesses and individuals-the mainstay of the mainland market-to seek to convert their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into dollars, potentially adding downward pressure on the domestically traded yuan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has tolerated a weaker yuan since early October, right after its entry into the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 's elite group of reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , acknowledging that a cheaper \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is the price of using easy money to prop up the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pace of depreciation has quickened since \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2 's surprise U.S. presidential-election win sent the greenback soaring and emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tumbling. The yuan is down 6.2% against the dollar this year in onshore markets, closing lower against the U.S. dollar Thursday. The dollar closed at 6.9189 yuan, compared with 6.8928 Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  is unwilling to let the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slide too far, too fast, for fear that might lead to destabilizing capital flows out of the country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors in offshore markets have, though, been pounding the yuan weaker, in the apparent belief that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may struggle to control the pace of its decline.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " to shore up the yuan, said Prashant Singh, a senior portfolio manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Neuberger Berman\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore. But he said he expects the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fall further because of the gloomy outlook for global trade and the diverging economic trajectories in the U.S. and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -led \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are typically temporary in nature," Mr. Singh added. "The fundamental factors still point to weakness."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro inched up against the dollar on Thursday after closing at its lowest against the dollar since March 2015, pressured both by the appreciation of the dollar and concerns over political risks in the eurozone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area's economy could be affected by U.S. trade policy changes and possible spillover effects from higher inflation and U.S. interest rate expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economic policies introduced by Mr. Trump "will likely become more inward-oriented," the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  wrote in its biannual Financial Stability Review. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US TREASURYS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. markets were closed Thursday for the Thanksgiving holiday. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets calmed Thursday after Wall Street climbed to record highs for a third consecutive session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Stoxx Europe 600 edged up 0.3% Thursday, following a mixed session in Asia. U.S. markets were closed for the Thanksgiving holiday. The WSJ Dollar Index had gained slightly Thursday afternoon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average, the S&P 500 and the Russell 2000 all closed at record highs in light trading on Wednesday, while the dollar climbed to its highest since 2002 on expectations for stronger U.S. growth and higher interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The reflationary theme is sucking money back into the U.S. stock market and injecting huge shock waves through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets," said Jane Foley, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , referring to expectations that tax cuts and less regulation will boost corporate profits and U.S. growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest leg higher followed strong U.S. economic data on Wednesday including durable goods orders, a measure of consumer sentiment and fresh hints the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  would likely raise interest rates in December.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed officials said a rate rise was possible "relatively soon" if coming data continued to show an improving economy, minutes from the bank's November meeting showed. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian markets were broadly lower Thursday, in response to strong economic data out of the U.S. overnight that raised the odds of rate increases, dollar strengthening and more capital flight from Asia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Durable goods orders in the U.S. rose 4.8% in October from a month earlier, well above the 2.7% gain predicted by economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal. In addition, a measure of U.S. consumer sentiment rose in November, signaling rising confidence in the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 closed nearly flat, Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index closed 0.3% lower and South Korea's Kospi fell 0.8%. Japan's Nikkei, however, gained 0.9% for the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The data pointed to likely improved U.S. demand for goods and commodities from Asia-Pacific.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I was very surprised to see strong European PMIs [purchasing managers indexes] and very strong U.S. data as well...it's boding well for risk appetite," said Christoffer Moltke-Leth, director of global sales trading at Saxo Capital Markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But more significantly for markets, the data increased expectations for interest-rate rises in the U.S., ramping up angst about more capital flight out of emerging Asia as traders chase higher yields in the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There are concerns about emerging markets," said Mr. Moltke-Leth, pointing to Malaysia, Indonesia and the Philippines.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Minutes from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's November meeting indicated most officials believe a rate increase could become appropriate "relatively soon" if data continued to show an improving economy. This has seen the U.S. dollar strengthen against many \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Asia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indonesian rupiah was off 0.5% Thursday, the Japanese yen slipped 0.7% and the Malaysian ringgit was 0.4% lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese stocks benefited from the weaker yen, which boosts the competitiveness of the country's exporters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yen weakness continued to be "very favorable" for Japan stocks, said Hisao Matsuura, chief strategist at Nomura Japan. He expected Japanese firms would post positive earnings revisions as early as next quarter. "How much they recover will show how much things have improved," Mr. Matsuura said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Toyota Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares closed up 4.8%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nissan Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  was up 2.6% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honda Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  climbed higher at 3.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong stocks slid as continued yuan slippage hurt offshore interest in Chinese companies listed in the city.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixed the yuan 0.26% weaker against the U.S. dollar on Thursday. It has guided the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weaker for most of this month amid broad dollar strengthening.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The [yuan] depreciation will be more negative to Chinese companies that have offshore [listings]," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alexander Lee\f28 \uc2 \f2 , research director at DBS Vickers. "These tend to be China properties, airlines and some of the environmental companies."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper, however, has been a bright spot. The industrial metal jumped, buoyed by positive U.S. economic data and expectations that U.S. President-elect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  will spend heavily on infrastructure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This has boosted shares of copper stocks in China, including Jiangxi Copper, closed up 6.4% in Hong Kong trade Thursday, and Yunnan Copper, finished up 4.6% on the Shenzhen bourse.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asia-Pacific government bond yields rose Thursday, tracking the movement of U.S. Treasurys as market expectations for a December Fed rate increase stayed strong. Yields rise as bond prices fall. The 10-year debt yield edged up for Singapore, Indonesia, Hong Kong, Thailand, South Korea and Malaysia. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian shares paused Thursday after a strong run so far this week, with modest gains by the major banks countered by a retreat in resources stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After climbing by 133 points over the last two days, the S&P/ASX 200 edged up 0.7 point to 5485.1.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial stocks continued to push higher, but mining and energy counters pulled back from sharp gains of late as commodity prices continued to rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  held steady, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia & New Zealand Banking\f28 \uc2 \f2  gained 0.6%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 0.5% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Australia Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 0.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BHP Billiton\f28 \uc2 \f2  was flat and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  was 0.3% higher, but iron-ore producer Fortescue Metals Group lost 1.3%, after closing Thursday at a multiyear high, and gold miner Newcrest Mining fell 3.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 24 Nov 2016 15:37 ET \par Australian Morning Briefing: U.S. Markets Closed -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Industrial metals have been on a run of late, with copper futures jumping again Thursday and iron ore prices closing in on $75 a metric ton on heightened optimism about demand from China as well as the U.S. in the wake of the presidential election. However, gold prices dropped to a nine-month low, extending losses as the U.S. dollar has strengthened.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among energy shares, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Woodside Petroleum\f28 \uc2 \f2  was down 0.3% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oil Search\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 1.4%. Oil futures were little changed in Asian trade Thursday after data showed a fall in U.S. crude inventories and ahead of next week's closely watched meeting of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEW ZEALAND STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand shares rose Thursday as stocks such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fisher & Paykel Healthcare\f28 \uc2 \f2  (FPH.NZ) and insurer Tower (TWR.NZ) rebounded from a heavy selloff in recent days and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Auckland International Airport\f28 \uc2 \f2  (AIA.NZ) continued to gain on Wednesday's strong traffic numbers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The NZX-50 index closed up 0.5% at 6883.25, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Auckland International Airport\f28 \uc2 \f2  rising 1.5% to NZ$6.285.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market rose during the session from a steady start after receiving little direction from the U.S. where stocks stabilized ahead of the Thanksgiving holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fisher & Paykel, which has been hit recently by concerns about litigation with \f28 \uc2 \f2 ResMed\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RMD.AU) over its sleep apnea devices, ended up 2.3% at NZ$8.34. Also among the gainers, Tower rose 2.7% to NZ$0.755, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 A2 Milk Company\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ATM.NZ) ended up 2.6% at NZ$2.37, extending recent gains after reporting that net profit in its first four months rose six-fold.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bucking the trend, Trustpower (TPW.NZ) fell 4.5% to NZ$4.49, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Warehouse Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (WHS.NZ) fell 2% to NZ$2.90 and Z Energy (ZEL.NZ) dropped 2.2% to NZ$7.08. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN & NZ CORPORATE & ECONOMIC CALENDAR: \par  \par Friday, November 25, 2016: \par  \par CORPORATE: \par Local/GMT/ET \par 0900/0100/2000 AUS Fleetwood Corp Ltd (FWD.AU) Full year 2016 AGM \par 0900/0100/2000 AUS Gindalbie Metals Ltd (GBG.AU) Full year 2016 AGM \par 1000/1000/0500 AUS Coal of Africa (CZA.AU) Full year 2016 AGM \par 1000/2100/1600 NZ Warehouse Group (The) (WHS.NZ) Full year 2016 AGM / Webcast \par 1000/2300/1800 AUS RCG Corp Ltd (RCG.AU) Full year 2016 AGM \par 1000/2300/1800 AUS Rex Minerals Limited (RXM.AU) Full year 2016 AGM \par 1000/1000/0500 AUS Range Resources Ltd (RRS.AU) Full year 2016 AGM \par 1100/0300/2200 AUS Imdex Limited (IMD.AU) Full year 2016 AGM \par 1100/0000/1900 AUS Primary Health Care Ltd Full year 2016 AGM \par (PRY.AU) \par 1100/0000/1900 AUS Select Harvests Limited Full year 2016 AGM \par (SHV.AU) \par 1100/0000/1900 AUS TBG Diagnostics Limited Full year 2016 AGM \par 1430/0630/0130 AUS BC Iron Ltd (BCI.AU) Full year 2016 AGM \par 1500/0700/0200 AUS Perseus Mining Ltd. (PRU.AU) Full year 2016 AGM \par N/A AUS Primary Health Care Ltd Trading statement \par (PRY.AU)* \par N/A NZ Kathmandu Holdings Ltd Full year 2016 Dividend payment date \par (KMD.NZ) \par  \par ECONOMIC/POLITICAL: \par Local/GMT/ET \par 1045/2145/1645 NZ Oct DELAYED: Overseas Merchandise Trade, date to be \par advised following recent earthquake in New Zealand \par  \par METALS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper was up 2.8% at $5,903.50 a metric ton in late Asia trade and prices hit a 17-month high on Thursday morning in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, the metal closed at a 16-month high in New York trading after U.S. durable goods data showed a 4.8% jump, the highest in a year, stoking further expectations of higher demand for the metal. American markets will be closed on Thursday for Thanksgiving.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold, meanwhile, was trading at its lowest level in 9 1/2 months on Thursday as the strong dollar and speculation about an Indian import ban continued to take the shine off the metal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For the short term, the precious complex will find it hard to recover with the dollar as strong as it is," David Govett, head of precious metals at brokerage firm Marex Spectron, wrote in a research note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Thursday afternoon, the WSJ Dollar Index, which weighs the dollar against a basket of other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was up 0.2%, building on a 14-year high on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger dollar is typically bearish for dollar-denominated commodities, because it makes them more expensive for buyers who hold other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But copper's rally--which began before the U.S. presidential election on the back of signs of strengthening Chinese demand and then picked up further momentum following \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2 's win--has so far shaken off the strong dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "These are unprecedented moves," said Matt France, head of institutional sales for Asia at brokerage Marex Spectron. "Global money managers are starting to put Dr. Copper back on their trading screens," he said, stoking further bullish momentum.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That appetite for the metal could extend into the new year, Mr. France added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other analysts were more skeptical that the pace of the rally could be sustained.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think the danger is [copper] has run ahead of itself," said William Adams, head of research at FastMarkets in London, who added that copper would likely see profit-taking ahead of the weekend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The metal is also in the midst of a "catch up" rally, Mr. Adams said. For most of the year, copper lagged behind the double-digit year-to-date gains in the other base metals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper is now up roughly 25% so far in 2016, surpassing aluminum and clocking roughly the same gains as lead, but the red metal is still trailing the sharp gains in the other base metals, particularly nickel and tin.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The other base metals were mixed on Thursday afternoon. Aluminum was down, lead was up, zinc was down, nickel was down, and tin was flat. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OIL FUTURES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent crude, the global oil benchmark, gained 0.10% to $49.00 a barrel on London's \f28 \uc2 \f2 ICE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Futures exchange, with all eyes on the coming \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\f28 \uc2 \f2  meeting next week, where a deal to cut output is expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Headlines on \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  have been the dominant factor driving price moves in the oil market for much of this year. Prices have rebounded in recent weeks in anticipation that the cartel will follow through with a production cut at its Vienna meeting on Nov. 30, though some sticking points remain around the terms of a cut for members such as Iran and Iraq.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With a steady flow of supportive statements, member countries have raised the stakes and put themselves in a position where something needs be delivered," consultancy JBC Energy said in a research note, adding that a deal remains complex due to economic challenges in member states and geopolitical issues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most analysts expect a swift fall in oil prices if \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  fails to reach a deal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The consequence of no deal will be well known to all, and one that none can afford, even the Saudis," said Wellington, New Zealand based Stuart Ive, private client manager at OM Financial.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to calculations by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Saudi Arabia requires an oil price of $74 per barrel if it is to halt the decline in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Many \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  members attempted to offset their revenue losses by expanding output, but in doing so simply increased the oversupply," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a research note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Data on Wednesday showed that U.S. weekly crude stocks fell by 1.3 million barrels, surprising many analysts who had expected stocks to grow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the number of rigs drilling for oil rose by three in the past week to 474, according to services company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baker Hughes Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The rig count has been rising since the beginning of summer, after U.S. oil prices recovered to above $50 a barrel, having dipped to around $26 in February. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES OF THE DAY: \par Islamic State Claims Bombing in Iraq That Targeted Iranian Pilgrims \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Islamic State claimed a massive truck bombing that Iraqi authorities said killed 60 people south of Baghdad, including more than two dozen Iranian Shiite Muslims returning home after a religious pilgrimage. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Anbang Insurance\f28 \uc2 \f2  Plans First International Bond Issue \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anbang is pursuing its first international bond offering as the acquisitive Chinese insurer raises money to fund its ambitions to grow abroad and at home, according to people familiar with the situation. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Ctrip, China's Biggest Travel Site, Goes Abroad for Its Latest Expansion \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the acquisition of British search company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Skyscanner\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Ctrip, whose expansion has been fueled by the Chinese middle class's ever-growing appetite for travel, will grow its own footprint overseas. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Indonesia Near Tax Deal for $73 Million or Less \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Alphabet's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  unit is nearing a settlement with Indonesian authorities over back taxes for no more than $73 million, a senior tax official said, far less than the up to $367 million Indonesia initially sought. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par South Korean Scandal Widens With New Raids, Impeachment Effort \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean prosecutors searched the offices of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lotte Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 SK Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  as part of their widening probe into an influence-peddling scandal involving President Park Geun-hye and her confidant Choi Soon-sil. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Goldman Seeks $2 Billion for South Korean Asset \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  is shopping one of South Korea's biggest industrial-gas producers to prospective buyers in a deal that could fetch $2 billion. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Turkey Blames Damascus for Strike That Killed Soldiers in Syria \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turkey's military said three of its soldiers were killed in Syria, blaming the Assad regime for a deadly airstrike that would mark Damascus's first assault on Turkish forces since Ankara launched its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Syria. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Saudi Arabia Backs OPEC Push to Make Steep Oil Output Cuts \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Saudi Arabia is backing an effort to make the steepest oil-production cuts possible at \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's meeting next week and to convince producers outside the cartel to help remove almost 2% of the world's oil supply, people familiar with the matter said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU Votes to Freeze Talks on Turkey Joining the Bloc \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Parliament\f28 \uc2 \f2  voted overwhelmingly to temporarily freeze talks on Turkey's bid to join the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , citing deteriorating human rights and democratic standards under President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's rule. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Scaffolding Collapses at China Power Plant, Killing 67 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 24 Nov 2016 15:37 ET \par Australian Morning Briefing: U.S. Markets Closed -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The collapse of construction scaffolding at a power plant under expansion in eastern China killed at least 67 people, state media said, the latest deadly accident in a country grappling with poor industrial safety. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Retailers Push Early Start to Black Friday Sales \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the battle for shoppers, traditional retailers have been moving discounts earlier in November, linking their stores and websites more closely and finding ways to capitalize on the rise in mobile shopping. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Trump Allies Raise Doubts About Mitt Romney Leading State Department \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fight over the Trump administration's secretary of state appointment has spilled into the open, with some prominent Republicans close to President-elect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  arguing for a loyal supporter to fill the post rather than longtime critic Mitt Romney, currently a front-runner for the job.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 24, 2016 15:37 ET (20:37 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161124ecbo0020d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
