\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Dollar pulls back after skittling world  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 980 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2016\par 17:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Dollar index near 14-year high after upbeat U.S. data\par \par \uc2 * Yen and euro pressured\par \par \uc2 * Emerging market central banks seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Yuan near 7 per dollar level for first time since May 2008\par \par \uc2 * India's rupee at record low, Turkish lira steadies on rate hike\par \par \uc2 * Prospects of China, U.S. inflation buoy copper, zinc\par \par \uc2 * Crude oil little changed amid \uc2 OPEC\uc2  meeting uncertainty\par \par \uc2 By Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 24 (Reuters) - The dollar surged to a near 14-year high before pulling back on Thursday, clocking up records against a range of other top world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and skittling emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Stronger data from the world's biggest economy had underpinned the greenback's gains, which were further amplified by thinner volumes as U.S. traders stayed away for the Thanksgiving holiday.\par \par \uc2 It was off its highs as Europe wound down but had earlier pushed its way past more of last year's peaks against the euro to reach $1.0515, with only the March 2015 high of $1.0457 standing in the way of a drive towards parity.\par \par \uc2 The yen had skidded to an eight-month low and China's yuan to an 8-1/2 year low, while the highly sensitive Turkish lira and Indian rupee hit new troughs as warning lights flashed in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 "There doesn't seem to be anything stopping U.S. yields going higher in the near-term so I think people are going to stay on the dollar trend," \uc2 State Street Global Markets\uc2 ' head of global macro strategy, Michael Metcalfe, said.\par \par \uc2 "The only risk to this are that the dislocations in markets outside of the U.S., particularly in emerging markets, get to a point where they start to feed back into concerns (for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  as it looks to raise interest rates)," he said.\par \par \uc2 In contrast to all the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  noise, European shares saw a broadly quiet day, with most of the main bourses inching up on gains from chemical and insurance sector stocks but capped by weaker banks.\par \par \uc2 German business confidence data showed firms remained unfazed, for now at least, by the U.S. election win for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the political uncertainty bubbling in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 However, the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  delivered an unusually downbeat message, warning that global political shifts could compound existing vulnerabilities to rising interest rates and revive worries about the euro zone's weaker economies.\par \par \uc2 ECB Vice President \uc2 Vitor Constancio\uc2  said the bank would be watching Italy particularly closely as it braces for a referendum on sweeping changes to its constitution next month.\par \par \uc2 "It's the sort of political uncertainty that will trigger or not an economic shock in financial markets," Constancio told reporters after presenting the \uc2 ECB\uc2 's twice yearly report on financial stability, refering to if Italy's government losses the vote.\par \par \uc2 "And depending on the degree of that shock, then we have to see if we have anything to do or not."\par \par \uc2 DOLLAR BULLS IN THE CHINA SHOP\par \par \uc2 It was enough to keep bond markets playing the transatlantic divide that has been widening again on bets that, while the United States may be about to raise interest rates, Europe is unlikely to follow suit for a couple of years.\par \par \uc2 The yield on Germany's 10-year government bond, the benchmark for the region, fell 2 basis points (bps) to 0.26 percent, while Italy, which has been plagued by its political concerns, outperformed with yields down 5 bps to 2.08 percent.\par \par \uc2 In the United States on Wednesday by contrast, the two-year Treasury yield hit its highest since April 2010.\par \par \uc2 The firm dollar hit most emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with China's yuan nearing the 7 per dollar level for the first time since May 2008.\par \par \uc2 State banks or \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , India, Indonesia and the Philippines were all suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the slide in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's lira and India's rupee both sank to record lows. The lira was also buffeted by calls from European lawmakers to halt Turkey's \uc2 EU\uc2  membership talks, though it clawed back some ground after the \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised one of its key interest rates for the first time since 2014.\par \par \uc2 "Exchange rate movements due to recently heightened global uncertainty and volatility pose upside risks on the inflation outlook," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy committee said in its statement.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan lost 0.4 percent, though the drop in the yen lifted the export-orientated Nikkei in Tokyo to a near 11-month high.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng shed 0.2 percent while higher metals prices lifted China's blue-chip CSI300 index 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices were little changed amid all the dollar commotion and ahead of a planned \uc2 OPEC\uc2 -led cut in crude production at a meeting on Nov. 30.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude was up 10 cents at $48.08 a barrel and Brent was up a similar amount at $49.09.\par \par \uc2 Industrial metals remained red-hot meanwhile on hopes of a revival in U.S. manufacturing and infrastructure spending under Trump. London zinc hit an 8-year high and copper jumped for a fourth day in a row to put $6,000 a tonne within reach.\par \par \uc2 "Strong durable goods orders in the U.S. helped buoy investors who have viewed Trump's upcoming presidency as a positive for industrial metals demand," ANZ said in a report.\par \par \uc2 For Reuters new Live Markets blog on European and UK stock markets see reuters://realtime/verb=Open/url={\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://emea1.apps.cp.extranet.thomsonreuters.biz/cms/?pageId=livemarkets"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://emea1.apps.cp.extranet.thomsonreuters.biz/cms/?pageId=livemarkets}}}\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Melanie Burton in Melbourne and Balazs Koranyi and Francesco Canepa in Frankfurt; Editing by Louise Ireland and Toby Chopra) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-24T17:37:36.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161124ecbo00rh7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
