\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Dollar pulls back after skittling world  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 884 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2016\par 13:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Dollar index near 14-year high after upbeat U.S. data\par \par \uc2 * Yen and euro pressured\par \par \uc2 * Emerging market central banks seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Yuan near 7 per dollar level for first time since May 2008\par \par \uc2 * India's rupee, Turkish lira at record low after rate rise\par \par \uc2 * Prospects of China, U.S. inflation buoy copper, zinc\par \par \uc2 * Crude oil little changed amid \uc2 OPEC\uc2  meeting uncertainty\par \par \uc2 By Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 24 (Reuters) - The dollar surged to a near 14-year high before pulling back on Thursday, clocking up records against a range of other top world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and skittling emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Stronger data from the world's biggest economy and thinner volumes on the U.S. Thanksgiving holiday underpinned the dollar's gains.\par \par \uc2 The dollar had eased off highs by midday after pushing its way past more of last year's peaks against the euro to reach $1.0515, with only the March 2015 high of $1.0457 standing in the way of a drive towards parity.\par \par \uc2 The yen had skidded to an eight-month low and China's yuan to an 8-1/2 year low, while the highly sensitive Turkish lira and Indian rupee hit new troughs.\par \par \uc2 "There doesn't seem to be anything stopping U.S. yields going higher in the near-term so I think people are going to stay on the dollar trend," \uc2 State Street Global Markets\uc2 ' head of global macro strategy, Michael Metcalfe, said.\par \par \uc2 "The only risk to this are that the dislocations in markets outside of the U.S., particularly in emerging markets, get to a point where they start to feed back into concerns (for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  as it looks to raise interest rates)," he said.\par \par \uc2 In contrast to all the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  noise, European shares saw a broadly quiet day, with most of the main bourses inching up on gains from chemical and insurance sector stocks but capped by weaker banks.\par \par \uc2 German business confidence data showed firms remained unfazed, for now at least, by the U.S. election win for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the political uncertainty bubbling in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 However, the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  delivered an unusually downbeat message, warning that global political shifts could compound existing vulnerabilities to rising interest rates and revive worries about the euro zone's weaker economies.\par \par \uc2 "This in turn could delay much-needed fiscal and structural reforms and could in a worst-case scenario reignite pressures on more vulnerable sovereigns," it said. "In particular, concerns about debt sustainability might re-emerge despite relatively benign financial market conditions."\par \par \uc2 EMERGING STRAINS\par \par \uc2 It was enough to keep bond markets playing the transatlantic divide that has been widening again on bets that, while the United States may be about to raise interest rates, Europe is probably unlikely to follow suit for a couple of years.\par \par \uc2 The yield on Germany's 10-year government bond, the benchmark for the region, fell 2 basis points (bps) to 0.26 percent, while Italy, which has been plagued by political concerns ahead of a referendum on constitutional reform, outperformed with yields down 5 bps to 2.08 percent.\par \par \uc2 In the United States on Wednesday by contrast, the two-year Treasury yield hit its highest since April 2010.\par \par \uc2 The firm dollar hit most emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with China's yuan nearing the 7 per dollar level for the first time since May 2008.\par \par \uc2 State banks or \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , India, Indonesia and the Philippines were all suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the slide in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's lira and India's rupee both sank to record lows, though the lira clawed back some ground as its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised one of its benchmark interest rates for the first time since 2014.\par \par \uc2 "Exchange rate movements due to recently heightened global uncertainty and volatility pose upside risks on the inflation outlook," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy committee said in its statement.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan lost 0.4 percent, though the drop in the yen lifted the export-orientated Nikkei in Tokyo to a near 11-month high.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng shed 0.2 percent while higher metals prices lifted China's blue-chip CSI300 index 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices were little changed amid all the dollar commotion and ahead of a planned \uc2 OPEC\uc2 -led cut in crude production at a meeting on Nov. 30.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude was up 20 cents at $48.22 a barrel and Brent was at $49.19.\par \par \uc2 Industrial metals remained red-hot on hopes of a revival in U.S. manufacturing and infrastructure spending under Trump. London zinc hit an 8-year high and copper jumped for a fourth day in a row to put $6,000 a tonne within reach.\par \par \uc2 "Strong durable goods orders in the U.S. helped buoy investors who have viewed Trump's upcoming presidency as a positive for industrial metals demand," ANZ said in a report.\par \par \uc2 For Reuters new Live Markets blog on European and UK stock markets see reuters://realtime/verb=Open/url={\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://emea1.apps.cp.extranet.thomsonreuters.biz/cms/?pageId=livemarkets"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://emea1.apps.cp.extranet.thomsonreuters.biz/cms/?pageId=livemarkets}}}\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Melanie Burton in Melbourne and Balazs Koranyi in Frankfurt; Editing by Louise Ireland) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-24T13:48:36.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161124ecbo00kgx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
