\par\par \b \uc2 Signs of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  in the Yuan?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lingling Wei and Carolyn CuiAND CAROLYN CUI \par \uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2016\par 01:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Some state-owned Chinese banks may have supported the yuan on Wednesday, temporarily helping to stabilize the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it dropped to its lowest level in eight years, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said.\par \par \uc2 There were signs that these Chinese banks sold dollars in the market during Wednesday's late-afternoon trading, the traders said. While they added that there was no indication of any direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , these state-owned banks often act as a proxy for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say.\par \par \uc2 The limited-scale dollar sales by the state banks showed that Chinese authorities preferred an orderly depreciation of the yuan rather than one driven by market forces, traders said.\par \par \uc2 During New York trading hours, the yuan had weakened as much as 1% to 6.9223 per dollar on Wednesday, but ended the day 0.9% stronger -- the largest one-day percentage gain since February -- to 6.7950 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 But the banks' moves have not been able to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. On Thursday, the yuan continued to lose value against the dollar, down 1.2% to 6.8748.\par \par \uc2 Analysts still expect the yuan to weaken further by the end of this year. But if it moves too fast, it will spook investors and trigger more capital outflows, said Win Thin, global head of emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman & Co\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's surprise victory has triggered a powerful rally in the dollar, driven by higher interest rates and growth expectations. This has put tremendous pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in emerging countries whose economies are tied to U.S. and reliant on dollar debt. President-elect Trump's harsh rhetoric against China during the campaign also made the yuan a target.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's recent declines also have roots in China's slowing economic growth. A weaker yuan is the price of using easy money to prop up growth and the rapid run-up in property prices in many cities is sending more Chinese looking to foreign markets to park their money, exerting pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts and policymakers said.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 18-11-16 0047GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020161118ecbi0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
