\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China commercial banks'  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  sales ease to $14.6 bln in Oct\b0\par\par\uc2 305 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2016\par 09:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quotes)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Nov 16 (Reuters) - China's commercial banks sold a net $14.6 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October, down from September's $28.4 billion as capital outflows eased, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "The pressure on cross-border capital outflows eased somewhat in October," the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement on its website.\par \par \uc2 China's cross-border capital flows are expected to stabilise over the medium- and long- term as the economy shows increased signs of steadying, it said.\par \par \uc2 For the January to October period, comemrcial net \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales stood at $258 billion, the regulator said.\par \par \uc2 Earlier data showed \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a net $39.2 billion worth of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October, easing from an eight-month high hit in September but still indicating continued official \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell $45.7 billion in October to $3.121 trillion, the biggest monthly decline since January.\par \par \uc2 Beijing has been trying to stem the flow of capital abroad with a string of measures aimed at closing loopholes and clamping down on illegal transfers, but pressure is growing again as the yuan weakens to eight-year lows against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Even as authorities have been busily damming up official channels for money to leave China, more than ever is leaking out through shady means, such as faked trade transactions through Hong Kong, financial industry executives say.\par \par \uc2 Expectations that the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  will raise interest rates in December has boosted the dollar, weighing on the yuan which has fallen nearly 5.5 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kevin Yao; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-16T09:21:58.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161116ecbg00cq8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
