\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan Declines to Near an 8-Year Low -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2016\par 08:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B18\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 PBOC\uc2  seems content as dollar strengthens against a number of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Shen Hong \f28 \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI -- The yuan fell to its lowest level against the dollar in nearly eight years Tuesday, continuing a recent drop that has accelerated since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's victory in the U.S. presidential election.\par \par \uc2 The value of one dollar rose as high as 6.8660 yuan in China's tightly controlled domestic market during the Asian trading morning, its highest point since Dec. 9, 2008, during the depths of the global financial crisis. The yuan's fall came after \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set its daily reference rate for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 6.8495 Tuesday, up 0.3% from the day before, marking the highest so-called yuan "fix" since Dec. 8, 2008. The yuan is allowed to trade 2% either side of the fix in mainland Chinese markets.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended Tuesday's trading in Asia at 6.8530 against the dollar, down from 6.8409 Monday.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  has allowed the yuan to weaken for the last eight consecutive trading days, with the pace of depreciation picking up since Friday when the dollar intensified its global surge following Mr. Trump's election victory.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump was often critical of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy during the presidential election campaign, saying he would brand the country a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator" when he took office, among other measures designed to combat China's alleged unfair trade practices.\par \par \uc2 Still, despite the looming Trump presidency, mainland traders said \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears content to let the yuan keep sliding for now, with the dollar strong against a number of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Our impression is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is happily going with the flow and it appears to have abandoned a heavy-handed market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  approach," said Liu Dongliang, senior analyst at \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Merchants Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  stepped in only after the dollar breached 6.86 but the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't as forceful as it used to be and is meant to pre-empt undesirable panic in the market," said a Shanghai-based head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at a domestic bank.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's weakness has come amid even heavier losses for many other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Mr. Trump was elected. Concerns about rising U.S. interest rates and trade protectionism have hit the value of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Mexico to Turkey and Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 "There's no point exhausting China's foreign reserves on defending the yuan when the dollar is surging internationally," Mr. Liu said.\par \par \uc2 Citing increased uncertainty over China's economy and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy posed by Mr. Trump's presidency, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  said in a recent research report that the bank has raised its forecast for the dollar-yuan exchange rate at the end of this year to 6.90 from 6.80 and at the end of next year to 7.20 from 6.90, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be happy to allow the yuan to fall to help Chinese exports remain competitive amid a broader economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 But Beijing's stance on the yuan also reflects its recent shift toward managing the yuan with reference to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its major trading partners.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the yuan's recent fall against the dollar, its value against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket has in fact risen in the same period," said Shuang Ding, China economist at Standard Chartered Bank.\par \par \uc2 According to the latest data, the official yuan index compiled by the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading System, or CFETS, a subsidiary of the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that oversees all onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, stood at 94.33 Friday, up from 93.78 a week earlier. The CFETS index reflects the yuan's performance against all the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded onshore, but on an undisclosed trade-weighted basis.\par \par \uc2 "At volatile times like this, the authorities are looking for an anchor to generate stability in the market and this index appears to be the one, " said Mr. Ding.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese authorities have attempted to direct investors' attention toward the strong performance of the yuan against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket and away from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 A commentary published on CFETS's website on Nov. 4 by a "special commentator" explained that the yuan's depreciation against the dollar was a natural move.\par \par \uc2 "While the yuan has depreciated against the dollar, its exchange rate against the basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has held basically stable and even appreciated slightly, which shows that the market in general has no worries about the yuan's exchange rate," said the commentary.\par \par \uc2 Write to Shen Hong at hong.shen@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 16, 2016 02:33 ET (07:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161116ecbg000it\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
